## CHAPTER — editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-01.md

# Chapter 1 — Week Three

The pages stopped.

Cael heard it before he understood what he had heard. All afternoon the archive had been full of one small sound, a page lifted and laid over and then the next page lifted and laid over, as steady as a clock in another room, because Karis turned pages the way Wray counted passes. An hour ago he had stopped hearing it, the way a person stops hearing rain. Now it was gone, and the quiet it left behind had a shape.

He looked up from his own sheet. It was a letter to nobody yet. He had been trying for most of a week to write a second letter to Vell, one that did not sound like a boy asking whether his first had arrived, and every version he tried came out sounding exactly like that.

At the high desk by the door, Prynn had looked up too.

That was the thing he would remember afterward. Prynn did not look up for anything. She had not looked up when the boxes came back from the delegation under their wax, or when a first-year knocked a lamp off a reading stand in the autumn, or when the Compact's own officer had walked the length of this room with his cases in his hands and his aide two steps behind him. She looked up now. She looked down the long table at Karis, and her pen stayed lifted over the restoration ledger, and she waited.

Karis was sitting very still with one finger on a page.

The book in front of her was one of the founding compilations, a squat grey thing with boards gone soft at the corners. It had come back up the hill in the second cart, and Prynn had shelved it with her own hands three days before the term began. Karis had been working through its run since the first morning of the first week, and she had a method for it that Cael had watched long enough to know by heart. She read the table of contents, then the index, then every page in order from the first, and she copied out anything at all that used the old language, the language from before the schedule had a name for everybody, into the notebook with the brown ink.

She was not copying now.

"Karis?"

She did not answer him. She read the four lines under her finger again, slowly. Her lips moved on one word, and he was close enough to hear it, though barely.

"Assay."

Then she slid a slip of paper into the page and closed the book on it, and laid both hands flat on the cover. She breathed out through her nose for a long time.

"What is it?"

"Not yet," said Karis.

"You found something."

"I found four lines." She looked at him across the table, and her face was the careful face, the one she wore when she had a thing and did not trust it yet to hold her weight. "Four lines in a book older than this academy, in a section nobody has indexed since it was printed. That isn't a finding. It's a place to stand while I look for a finding." She tapped the cover once with two fingers. "If I tell you what it says, you'll start building on it. You won't mean to. You'll start, and Lira will start, and Brom will do a sum about it in his pocket book. In a fortnight there'll be a house on top of these four lines, and if they don't hold, the whole house comes down on all of us." She shook her head. "Not yet."

He knew better than to push. He had learned the shape of Karis's *not yet* across a whole winter. It was not a refusal. It was a date she had not set.

Down the room, Prynn lowered her pen and wrote something in the ledger. Cael would have given a good deal to know what.

He let his eyes go past her, down the long aisle between the cases, to the second-to-last case against the old wall. The traveller registers stood in their run on the third shelf, crosshatched spines in a row, and between two of them was the space the width of one volume. Nobody had closed it in the three weeks since. Nobody, he was sure now, ever would while Prynn was alive to stop them.

He did not know what Karis had just found. He did know, sitting at the long table with an unwritten letter under his hand, that the room had changed temperature, and that Prynn had felt it before he had. He sat with that a while. In two years of watching people for a living he had grown used to being the first in any room to notice a thing, and it was a strange, not unpleasant feeling to have been beaten to it by an old woman who never left her desk.

Outside, through the high window, he could see a slice of the gate and the end of the training hall. The man on the bench by the training hall door was there, as he was there every afternoon, with his hands on his knees and his hat on the bench beside him. He was one of five, and he did not trouble to hide, and neither did the other four. Nobody hid any more. That had been true since the ruling, and Cael had stopped flinching at it somewhere around the second week of the recess, which he counted as progress.

---

Karis told them that night, at the scarred table under the bricked-up hay door, and she told them in the form of terms.

"I'll say the word once," she said. "Here. Then nobody says it outside the four of us. Not in the yard, not in a letter home, not to Quenna, not to Naveth. Not even to Prynn, though Prynn knows already, because Prynn knows everything that happens in her room before it happens."

"Agreed," said Lira, without waiting to hear the word.

Brom put his spoon down. "Agreed."

Cael nodded.

"Assay," said Karis.

Brom frowned at his barley. "That's a mint word."

"It's a mint word," Karis agreed. "It's a smith's word too, and an assayer's, which is the same word in an apron. Somebody brings you a piece of metal that claims to be silver. You don't take the stamp's word for it, or the merchant's. You put it to the test, and what it does under the test tells you what it is." She had her oldest notebook open, the one with the brown ink. "There's a clause in a founding-era compilation that uses the word about people. A charter clause, four lines long. It sets out how a chartered academy could once evaluate a practitioner for whom no registry classification existed."

Nobody said anything for a moment. At the far end of the stable somebody was arguing cheerfully about whose turn it was at the woodpile, and the fire popped, and none of the four of them heard any of it.

"Evaluate how?" said Cael.

"By demonstration, before the academy's own assessors." Karis did not look up from the page. "You'd show them what you could do, and they'd write down what you showed them, and the academy would carry you on its rolls on the strength of what was written. It's a standing. It isn't an exception, and it isn't a loan of some category that was built for somebody else and stretched to fit. It's a standing of its own, with its own name."

Lira had put her chin on her hand. Her eyes had gone very bright.

"So it's a door," she said.

"It's a hole in a wall that might once have been a door." Karis closed the notebook. "And I'm going to spend the rest of this term trying to brick it up."

Brom looked at her. "Why would you do that?"

"Because the schedule was rewritten long after that compilation was printed. There was a standardization directive, a few hundred pages of it, and it swept out the old vocabulary on purpose, because that was what it was written to do. Clauses like this one were exactly what it was aimed at. If it repealed this clause by name anywhere, then the clause is dead, and it doesn't matter how pretty it is." Her eyes went from one of them to the next. "I don't want to find out it's dead in front of a magistrate. I want to find out here, at this table, in the dullest way there is. So I'm going to try to kill it every way I can think of, and if after all that it still won't die, then it's a door."

Lira considered this. "And if the bricks don't take?"

"Then I'll stop being careful," said Karis, "and you can all start building houses."

Cael sat back in the lamplight. He found that he wanted it to be a door so badly that his hands had gone still on the table, the way they went still before a bout he cared about. He noticed that, and he did not like it.

Last term he had stood up in a room full of people and read their own schedule back to them, from the first page to the last, and he had won because he had not reached past the text by a single line. If he let himself start wanting this clause to be alive, he would end by reading it the way the Compact's clerks had read his own file for two years: hoping, and writing the hope down as though it were a finding, and never once noticing the difference.

"Then I'll help you kill it," he said.

Karis looked at him.

"I mean it." He turned his cup on the wood. "If it can be killed, I'd rather it died in your notebook than in somebody else's courtroom with my name on the paper. Tell me what to read. I'll read it looking for the line that ends it, not the line that saves it."

Something in Karis's face eased by a hair.

"That's the only kind of help that's any use to me," she said. "Thank you."

Lira knocked her shoulder against his. Brom took out his small pocket book and wrote something in it. When Cael raised his eyebrows, Brom turned the page round. It said *Week three. K. found a word. We're trying to kill it.* Under it was a single tally mark.

"Day one," said Brom. "I'll keep the count."

---

His own term ran on stone and on Wray's count, and it ran whether or not anybody had found a word.

On the first morning back she had put him on the defensive floor with Hobb at the north mark and watched him run the framework at panel speed, four passes, without a word. On the second morning she broke the count on the third pass, as she always broke it, and said, "Pivot." On the third morning she did not say it. He had spent the whole of the recess on that pivot: on the frozen yard behind the residence wing, on the cold boards of his own room, in the dark before the first bell, turning on the ball of the foot again and again and feeling for the exact place where the weight came over late. A tenth of a beat was not much. It was the difference between a slip that took him out of a strike and a slip that took him half out.

By week three it was gone, and Wray had written nothing about it, which was how he knew she had noticed.

He counted what he carried the way he had counted it at the end of last term, at the long table in the archive, in ink. He did not need the binder for it now. He could run the list in his head between passes, and he often did, because a count he could only do with the book open was a count he did not really own.

Five fragments, and every one of them came with a price.

The Wind-adjacent came first, Lira's, the one his feet reached for before he had chosen anything at all. He held half a breath at the front of every burst, and at the back of it he paid the landing beat: the locked half-breath when he could not change direction, which he had never once in two years been able to shorten. The landing came home through both heels and up the leading hip. On stone, the hip would take three bursts in an evening and say nothing. The fourth came due the next morning as a bright line from the crest of the hip down the inside of the thigh to the knee, and he had learned to believe that line the way he believed a ledger.

Then the Pressure-adjacent, which he did not use. The Iron-adjacent read, Brom's, which laid mass and direction on the skin of his forearms a pace and a half out and never once told him what anybody meant to do. The Compression-adjacent, which took a push and sent it down through his bones into the floor. The Ember-adjacent last and newest, which let go of fire at a single point and cost him the same whatever size he asked for.

Five things, and not one of them a Path. A Path had a tier and a rank, ten ranks to a tier, and a declaration that rose in front of the eyes when the Arbiter judged a practitioner had earned it, and a registry line that told a stranger what to expect before the stranger had seen anything. He had a list in a binder and a set of prices. The prices were true, and three people on this hill knew all of them, and that would have to do.

Edran found him at the rail on the Thursday of week three.

He did not come over to talk. He came over with a sheet of paper, folded once, and held it out. The hand on it was Wray's.

*Exhibition bout, full sanction. Faculty supervised, on the record. Presiding: Instructor Wray. Second seat: Assessor Quenna. Eight exchanges on the instructor's count, standings rules. Week twenty, first bell, the training hall.*

"Week twenty," said Cael.

"I asked for week ten. She said I wasn't ready." Edran took the sheet back and folded it along its crease. "I asked for week fifteen. She said neither of us was." He looked out at the floor, where a pair of second-years were running the Shield drill with a great deal of effort and very little Shield. "She was right the first time. I don't know yet about the second."

"Same terms."

"Same terms."

He went back to his own chalk. Cael watched him settle into his stance at the far end of the hall and build a structure on his leading forearm, a hard pale shell like the first ice on a trough. It was the Glass stance Cael had learned at this rail in six days the autumn before, and beaten in the formal yard. It sat lower than it had then, by most of a hand, and the off arm rode forward a little, carried closer to the line of the body. He could see plainly that it was different. He could not yet see what the difference bought.

Hobb came and leaned on the rail beside him and watched the same thing for a long time.

"Lower," said Hobb.

"I saw."

"He'll be slower to reach you," said Hobb, and then thought about it some more, while Edran built a second shell and let it go in a spray of pale glitter. "Or he won't. I don't know which."

It was the most Cael had ever heard Hobb say about another fighter. He wrote it down that night under Edran's name, with the date, and drew a question mark in the margin beside it. Then he drew a second one, because one did not seem enough.

---

The letters went out in week four. There were nine of them.

Karis wrote them at the long table over three evenings, and she wrote every one of them twice, a draft first and then a fair copy with nothing crossed out. She chose the nine houses from a list she made out of Prynn's charter digests: the oldest chartered academies she could find, whose founding papers had been written early enough to be in the old language and might still carry it somewhere in their articles. To each of them she put the same question in the same plain words. Did their charter, or any compilation of charter law they held, include the clause, and was it anywhere recorded as repealed?

She did not use the word in the letters. She cited the clause by its compilation, its volume and its page.

"If they want to know what it says," she said, "they can go and read it. Anybody who can't be bothered to read four lines isn't somebody whose answer I want."

Prynn gave her the founding compilations without being asked twice. They came off the third shelf of the fifth case in their run of eleven, and Prynn laid them on the long table herself, one on top of the next, in order.

When Karis asked for the consolidations, Prynn did not move.

"Which consolidations?"

"All of them. Everything made after the directive that claims to set out the law as it stood afterward."

"Then ask in writing," said Prynn, "so that I know which you mean."

Karis wrote it. It took her most of an evening, because Prynn sent the first request back across the room with a single line drawn under the word *everything* and nothing else on it at all. The second request named eleven titles. Prynn read it, initialed it, and fetched seven of them. She came back to the table with a slip that accounted for the other four: two had never been bought, one had been lent to a provost in the year of the great flood and not returned, and one had gone to a binder in the regional town to be resewn and was not yet back.

Karis read the slip and looked up at Prynn, and Prynn looked back at her and said nothing whatever, and went back to her desk.

After that the work was slow, and most of it was Karis's, and Cael did what he had promised. He sat across from her and read for the line that would end it.

There were three places a repealed clause would have had to go. The first was the directive's own repeal schedule, a long numbered list at the back of the directive of everything it struck by name. The second was the registry's consolidated list of repeals, which some patient clerk had drawn up twenty years later out of everything struck by anybody. The third was the guild digest, in which the member guilds had printed, with some bad temper, every clause they had ever lost. Karis gave him the guild digest because it was the worst printed of the three and the likeliest to hide something in small type. He read it with a ruler under each line, as Vell had once taught him to read a ledger, every entry in order and none of them softer than any other. It took him nine evenings.

The clause was not in it.

The replies began coming up the hill in week seven. The first, from a house on the coast, said in a page and a half that it held no such compilation and wished her well in her studies. The second, from an academy in the north, said the clause was obsolete and that it knew of no academy that had used it in living memory, and Karis wrote *obsolete is not repealed* in the margin in pencil, very small. The third and the fourth said nothing useful at some length. The fifth came from a provost's secretary, and Karis read it aloud at supper because it was beautifully written and said nothing at all, and Lira laughed until she had to put her spoon down.

The sixth was different.

It came in week eight, on the Wednesday coach, in a small upright hand that did not waste a line. It did not answer her question. It asked four of its own. Where precisely had she found the clause, and in which printing? Had she compared the founding text against the version carried in the first consolidation, word for word? Was she aware that the compilation she cited had a known printer's error two pages further on, and had she satisfied herself that it did not reach back to this clause? And who, if anybody, had she told?

It was signed *Bracken, Registrar, Halcenvane Academy.*

Karis read it at the long table. Then she read it again. She leaned back until the chair creaked and studied the dark beams overhead.

"Well?" said Cael.

"He doesn't believe me," said Karis. Her voice had something in it he had not heard from her all term. "He doesn't believe me at all. He wants to kill it too."

She sounded happier than she had since the hearing. She answered the four questions that night, at length, and three of the answers needed new reading, and she went and did the reading before she would let herself seal the letter.

---

It took eleven weeks in the end, from week three to week fourteen, three evenings out of every five, at the long table with the compilations laid down one side and the consolidations down the other under the good lamp.

In the last month Karis did the slowest thing Cael had ever seen a person do on purpose. She took each consolidation and laid it open beside the founding compilation it claimed to consolidate, and went through the two together, page against page. Wherever the old text had a clause, she found the place in the new one where that clause ought to be and looked at what stood there instead. Usually it was the same clause in newer words. Sometimes it was a note that the clause had been struck, with the number of the schedule that struck it. Once or twice it was nothing at all, and in every one of those cases, so far, she had afterward found the strike in one of the three lists, and drawn a neat line under it, and gone on.

The assay clause was in the founding text. It was in the first consolidation, word for word. In the second consolidation, printed after the directive, it was gone. There was no strike in its place and no note and no number. It was simply not there, the way a coat stops hanging on a hook when somebody takes it down one autumn and forgets to put it back.

She checked the three lists again, every line, with Cael reading them a second time behind her.

It was on none of them.

On the Thursday night of week fourteen she laid her pen down square to the edge of the table and sat looking at the two books open side by side. Down the room, Prynn's lamp was still burning at the high desk. Lira had gone up to bed an hour before. Brom was asleep with his head on his arms at the far end of the table and his pocket book open under one big hand, at a page that was almost entirely tally marks.

"It wasn't repealed," said Karis.

She said it in her flattest voice, the one she kept for things she was sure of.

"It was orphaned. Nobody killed it. The directive swept out a whole generation of vocabulary, and every academy that used this clause stopped using it, because after the directive it looked old-fashioned and nobody wanted to look old-fashioned in front of an inspector. So it went quiet. The second consolidation dropped it because nobody was using it any more. But nobody who had the power to repeal it ever did, and there isn't a line anywhere that ends it." She laid her palm flat on the second consolidation. "The paperwork records the stopping. It doesn't record a killing, because there wasn't one."

Cael looked at the two books and the empty place in one of them where a clause had stood for decades before anybody stopped looking at it.

"What's the difference," he said slowly, "between a clause that's dead and one that everybody's stopped using?"

Karis turned her head and looked at him.

"Custom against text." She let it sit a moment. "You've won that one before."

He laughed. He could not help it; it came out of him far too loud for the archive, and Brom stirred at the end of the table without waking, and down the room Prynn's pen paused and then went on.

"I won it as a gap," he said. "I stood in a hole in their schedule and told them they couldn't reach me there."

"You did. And I told you a gap isn't anything you can put a house on." Karis picked her pen up again and drew a clean sheet toward her. "This isn't a gap. It's a clause in force, in a founding schedule, that a chartered academy can use if its own charter carries the old text forward under one of the standard articles. It's a floor." She dipped the pen. "So now I tell somebody. I tell a registrar in a house chartered a hundred and forty-one years ago, in the old language, from its first article to its last, who has spent six weeks trying his hardest to prove me wrong and has failed."

She wrote until the lamp guttered and she had to trim it, and then wrote on. In the morning Cael carried the letter down the hill in the inside pocket of his coat, past the man on the bench by the training hall door and the man at the gate, who both watched him go and neither of whom did anything about it. He gave it across the draper's counter to wait for the next coach.

The draper dropped it into the bag. Cael stood in the shop a moment longer than he needed to, looking at the bag hanging on its peg among the parcels and the seed returns. Then he went back up the hill to tell Brom he could stop counting.

---

## CHAPTER — editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-02.md

# Chapter 2 — Forty-One Questions

Bracken's fourth letter had come up the hill in week eleven, three weeks before the finding, and Karis had opened it at the long table and laughed out loud.

Cael had never heard her laugh at a document before. She had laughed at Lira, and at Brom's pocket book, and once, helplessly, at the provost's secretary's letter that said nothing in such lovely sentences. But a document was work, and Karis did not laugh at work. She sat with the sheets fanned in her hand and laughed with her shoulders, silently, and then put the letter down on the table and smoothed it flat with both palms as if it might try to get away.

"Forty-one," she said.

"Forty-one what?"

"Questions. Numbered." She turned the first sheet toward him. "He's numbered them, and grouped them, and put a heading over every group, and under the last group he's left a space for my answers with a ruled line in it." She shook her head. "Nobody has ever sent me a letter with a ruled line in it. I think I'm in love with his filing."

There were five groups. The first was about the founding text: which copies of it Prynn held, where each had come from, whether any had been corrected by hand and in what ink. The second was about the consolidations, and it went through them one by one, by title, and asked of each of them a question Karis had not yet thought to ask. The third was about the three repeal lists. The fourth was headed *The candidate*, and the fifth was headed *Matters I may have missed*, and contained only one question, which was whether there were any.

The second group sent Karis to Prynn's desk before she had finished reading it. Bracken wanted to know whether any of Greyvane's copies of the founding text had been corrected by hand, and if so, in what ink and by whom, and Karis did not know. She stood in front of the high desk with the letter in her hand and asked.

Prynn did not look up from her ledger. "The third copy," she said. "Case five, the run of eleven, the one with the green tape on the spine. Two corrections in the margin, both in iron-gall ink, both in the hand of the archivist before the archivist before me. Neither of them touches your clause." She turned a page. "He's right to ask. Tell him I said so."

Karis went back to the table and wrote it down word for word, including the last line, and Cael watched her decide whether to put it in the letter and decide that she would.

Karis took the first three groups for herself and pushed the fourth across the table to Cael.

"These are yours. He's asking about your papers. I can't answer for you, and I wouldn't if I could." She hesitated. "Answer them as if he were going to stand you up in front of a magistrate with them. Because one day somebody will."

He read the fourth group through twice before he picked up a pen.

It wanted his name as the registry carried it. It wanted his number. It wanted the station where he had Kindled, and the date, and the designation the instrument had returned, written exactly as the station had written it, brackets and all. It wanted the name of the household he had been registered under and how that name was spelled. It wanted to know whether he had ever, at any academy, been enrolled under a category whose terms he could not honestly meet, and if so, under which category, and what had been done about it.

He answered them in order.

*Caelen Hesk-ward. 41-7843-V.* He wrote the number slowly, digit by digit, and checked it against the copy of the ruling in the binder, because he had learned that the number you were surest of was the one you were likeliest to get wrong. He wrote *Denvash* and the date. He wrote the designation in brackets, *[SHATTERED]*, as the station had written it, and under it a line saying that the panel at Greyvane had found it did not appear anywhere in the enumerated schedule, with the page of the ruling where it said so. He wrote *Hesk*, and then, because Bracken had asked how it was spelled, he wrote it again in capitals, and then felt foolish, and left it.

The last question took him longest.

He could have answered it in one word. The true word was *yes*. The question was not asking whether he had been honest with Greyvane. It was asking whether the category had fitted him, and it had not, and everybody at the table who had signed it had known that it did not.

He wrote *Yes.* Then he wrote the name of the category, *unclassified observer*, and the term he had been enrolled under it, and that the form had been defensible on its face and had been challenged by the Compact and held. And then, because a file that left out the uncomfortable part was only half a file, he wrote one more line.

*It held because its words were true. It did not describe me. Everyone who signed it knew that, including me.*

He read it back, and pushed the sheet across to Karis.

She read it once, and then she looked at him over the top of it for a long moment and did not say anything. She folded it into her answers with the rest. When the bundle went down the hill on the Wednesday coach, that line was in it, in his own hand, on its way to a man he had never met, and he found he did not mind at all.

---

The fifteenth letter came in week seventeen, and it had no questions in it.

By then there were a great many letters. Bracken did not wait for one answer before he wrote the next. He wrote by every coach, sometimes two letters in one packet, numbered on the outside so that Karis would read them in the order he had written them and not the order the post delivered them. Karis kept them in a bundle tied with grey string, which she had cut from the end of one of the delegation's boxes when they came back in the autumn and had been saving for something worth tying. The bundle was as thick as two fingers now.

The fifteenth was the thickest letter yet. Karis opened it at the long table after supper, with Lira on the bench by the door and Brom at the end of the table with his pocket book, and Cael across from her waiting to be handed his part. She read the first page. She turned to the second. She read that too, and the third, and then she went back to the first and read the whole thing again, much more slowly, with her finger moving down the margin.

Then she put it down and sat with her hands in her lap.

"Karis?" said Lira, from the door.

"He's done it himself," said Karis.

"Done what?"

"All of it. From the other end." She picked the first page up again. "I started from the founding schedule and worked forward. I took the clause and looked for every place after it where somebody could have killed it. He started from his own house. He took the article in Halcenvane's charter that carries the founding text into its own law, which was written in the old language a hundred and forty-one years ago, and worked backward from that, through every amendment his own academy ever made to its charter, to see whether any of them touched the clause. None of them did. Then he went to the directive and read it from the other end, not the repeal schedule but the transitional article, the part that says what happens to people enrolled under the old law before the new one came in." She touched the third page. "And it presupposes them. It says what to do with them. You can't make provision for people enrolled under a clause you've repealed. It doesn't mean anything."

She looked up at Cael.

"He came to the same place I came to, by a road I never thought of, without once asking me which road I'd taken. Two pages. *Orphaned, not repealed.* He used my word. He put it in quotation marks and wrote my name after it, as if I were somebody."

"You are somebody," said Brom.

"I'm a seventeen-year-old with a notebook." Karis's voice was not quite even. "He's a registrar. He doesn't have to agree with me. He doesn't have to do anything but answer the letters he wants to answer and file the rest." She laid the three pages square on the table. "Agreement is cheap. People agree with you because they like you, or because they're tired, or because they want you to go away. This is worth more than agreement. He got there by himself."

Cael looked at the three pages lying on the table between them.

He thought about what Bracken had in front of him, in an office he had never seen: a stranger's name, and a stranger's number, and a line in a stranger's hand that said *Everyone who signed it knew that, including me.* With that on his desk, Bracken had gone and done six weeks of work that nobody had asked him to do, to find out whether that stranger was standing on anything real. He had done it as if the answer mattered, and done it carefully, and then written down where he had got to and posted it four days down the road.

For two years everybody who had looked hard at Cael had been looking for the place where he would break. Coss had looked carefully. The registry had looked carefully. The careful looking had been aimed at him, the way a smith aims a hammer, and he had learned to stand still under it and say only true things. He realized, sitting at the long table, that he had never once until now been looked at that carefully on his own behalf.

It did not feel the way he would have expected. It felt like being cold for so long that the first warm room made your hands ache.

"You've gone red," said Lira, from the door.

"I haven't."

"You've gone red over a registrar's letter. I'm writing it down." She did not move from the bench. "Brom, write it down."

Brom wrote something in his pocket book and showed nobody what it was.

Karis answered the fifteenth letter that night, and it was the shortest letter she wrote all term. Cael watched her draft it three times. The first draft ran to a page and explained, at some length, the road she had taken and where it had crossed his. The second ran to half a page and thanked him. She tore both of them across and fed the halves to the stove at the far end of the archive, which Prynn permitted only for drafts.

The third draft was one line, and she let him read it before she sealed it.

*Agreed, by a different road. I'd rather have yours than my own. K. Dellenmoor.*

"That's all?" he said.

"He didn't ask me anything." Karis pressed the seal down with her thumb. "When somebody sends you a finding without a single question in it, the only polite thing to do is not to make them read a page of yours." She held the letter up to the lamp and looked at it. "Besides, I want him to know I can be short. He's going to be reading my letters for years.

---

The seal came in week nineteen, and it came to Naveth.

Cael heard about it the way he heard about everything that went on in the provost's office: first from the provost's clerk, who was pink with the importance of it, and then from Karis, who had been told by the clerk, and then from Naveth himself, who sent for him at the end of the afternoon and left his door wide open.

The office was as it always was, the long table piled with papers and the tall window looking down the hill. Naveth sat behind the table with a letter in front of him, flat on the blotter, under one long hand. The letter was folded in three, and where the folds met at the foot there was a seal in dark red wax, broken cleanly across.

"Halcenvane's counsel," said Naveth. "Their chancellor's own. She has gone through the whole architecture. Your friend's finding, the registrar's reconstruction, the charter article, the transitional provision, the lot. She has verified it, and she has put her seal on the verification and sent it to the institution that currently has you on its rolls. That is us." He did not move his hand. "Which is courtesy. She did not have to send it here at all."

"What does it say?"

"That the clause stands. That Halcenvane's charter carries it. That the chancellor of Halcenvane has directed her registrar to prepare an enrollment under it, effective at the start of their next term, on the satisfaction of an entry demonstration before their assessment office. That counsel finds no defect in the basis." He turned the letter round on the blotter so that it faced Cael. "Read it if you like. It is very short. It took them a great deal of money to make it that short."

Cael read it. It was very short. He did not understand all of it, and what he did understand was dry and exact and careful in the way the best of Karis's sentences were careful, never claiming one word more than it could carry. At the foot was a signature he could not read, and under it a line in another hand, *Seen. Withrow.*

He gave it back.

Naveth picked it up and read it again from the beginning. He had read it before Cael came in, Cael was sure; the clerk had said so. He read it now with his spectacles pushed up onto his forehead and the paper held a little away, as a man reads a letter he already knows and wants to see once more. His face, which Cael had watched break once on a witness floor and then mend, did something small and complicated.

Cael had seen that look on other faces, but never on Naveth's. It was the look of a man at a market stall, looking at a thing he had wanted all his life and could not afford, and finding he was glad somebody could.

Naveth had staked the whole of Greyvane on a form that held because its words were true and did not describe what it was holding. He had done it with a clerk and a provost's seal and an archive nobody had funded in sixty years. Halcenvane had a counsel of its own, and a registrar who wrote forty-one questions in a single letter for the pleasure of it, and money enough to make a thing short. And Naveth could see, better than anybody living, what every line of that letter had cost and what it had bought. He was not bitter about it. That was the remarkable thing. He was envious the way an honest man is envious, wanting the thing and not wanting it taken from anybody else.

He folded the letter along its creases and laid it down.

"It is the right house," said Naveth. "I want you to hear me say that, so that you never wonder afterward whether I said it through my teeth. Greyvane took you because it was the only door open, and because two people in it were stubborn. That was enough to get you through a winter. It is not enough to carry you through what comes after." He took his spectacles down off his forehead and folded them. "Halcenvane has a ladder and a library and counsel of its own. More to the point, it has something to prove, and it means to prove it with you. That isn't a kindness. You have had kindness. You need a house that wants you for a reason it can write down."

"You're telling me to go."

"I'm telling you it's yours to decide, and that if you decide to stay out of loyalty I will be very angry with you." The corner of his mouth moved. "Go away. I have a letter to answer at the last lawful hour, and for once I find I don't want to wait that long."

Cael went down the stair slowly.

He had known for weeks which way it would go. He had known, if he was honest, since the night in the archive when Karis laid her pen down square to the table. But knowing a thing and deciding it were different, and he had been careful all term to let them stay different until there was something on paper. Now there was. He stopped at the turn of the stair by the window, where he had read so many things that touched his own name, and looked down the hill at the road going away east.

He decided there, on the landing, with nobody to see it.

---

He told them at the scarred table that night, and nobody was surprised, and that was the best part of it.

"Halcenvane," he said. "Next term. If you'll come."

Lira looked at him as if he had said something very stupid.

"We filed in week sixteen," she said. "Brom and Karis and me. Transfers. Did you think we'd let you walk four days to a new gate on your own and find out from a letter which of us had come?" She speared a piece of turnip. "Karis wrote to the registrar. He sent the forms in the fourteenth letter. He said he'd assumed."

"He assumed," said Cael.

"He's a registrar," said Karis. "Assuming is what they do between filings. He also said he'd prefer all four of us at the gate on the same morning, because it'd save him a second walk to the lodge." She had her narrow brown notebook open, the watcher log, beside her bowl. "I've got a research standing to argue for. Archive access, terms written down. A library committee with a charter, he says, which means the terms can be read, and anything that can be read can be argued with."

"Standard enrollment," said Brom. "Me. I'll be the easy one again." He considered his barley. "I'm good at being the easy one. I've had practice."

Lira did not say what hers was. They all knew. Her papers would travel south ahead of her, the old ones from Fenmark and the new one from this hill. The new one was the best thing Greyvane had been able to give her, and Greyvane had never had the weight to make anybody act on it: a flag asking the registry to look at her tier again, signed by a provost who had watched her put Iron-equivalent fighters on the floor for two whole terms. It was the best Greyvane could do. Everybody at the table knew it was not enough. Her formal standing still said *Copper, Rank Two*, and a ladder read off formal standing.

Lira had wanted, for as long as Cael had known her, one thing that nobody had ever been willing to give her. It was not a rank, though she would take the rank. It was not a crown or a purse or a better room. It was to stand in front of the instrument that had read her at fourteen and set her down as a Copper practitioner, and to make it look again. She had said so once, on a road, in the dark, and never again, and he had not forgotten it.

"There's a ladder," said Lira, as if she had heard him think it. "Brom asked. A real one, with brackets, and boards up in a yard where everybody can read them, and a season." Her eyes went very bright and very hard. "Here they gave me two bouts in a closed yard and wrote me down as a flag. There, they'll have to watch me."

Cael looked round the table.

Four of them, and four tracks, and not one of the tracks the same. Two winters ago there had been no doors anywhere for any of them. Now each of them had a form in somebody's cabinet with their own name on it, and a reason on it, and a day.

Nobody said that they had outgrown Greyvane. Quenna had said it to him at the pump on the last free afternoon of the winter term, and it had been true then, and it was truer now. But it was a thing that felt ungrateful in the mouth, so none of them said it. They sat round the scarred table under the bricked-up hay door, where they had argued the whole of a hearing into shape, and ate their barley. Each of them, privately, was already counting the weeks.

"I'll miss the hay door," said Brom, after a while.

"It's bricked up," said Lira.

"That's what I'll miss," said Brom. "Somebody wanted it shut and did a good job of it."

---

Edran's stance kept him awake.

Through weeks fifteen to nineteen he watched it from the rail whenever he could, and it did not get any easier to read. Edran had rebuilt himself over the recess and through the whole of the term, an hour at a time, with Wray standing over him, and the man at the far end of the hall was not the one Cael had beaten in the formal yard.

The old Edran had led with his structures. He built a shell on the leading forearm and drove it forward, inviting a strike, because Glass wanted to be struck: a struck shell burst outward and shed the force, and the next shell formed out of the burst. The weakness had been the third. In a long sequence the third structure came up cold, and in the instant it took to come up there was a sliver of nothing on its edge where a fast hand could get in. Cael had found that sliver twice, and closed in it, and won.

The new Edran's third came up whole. Cael had seen it on the last free afternoon of the winter term and seen it every day since. But that was not what kept him awake. What kept him awake was the lower stance and the off arm carried forward, and the fact that Edran no longer drove his shells out to meet anything. He let them come to him. He had given up perhaps a hand of reach to do it, and Cael could not see what the hand had bought him.

"Fight him," said Lira, on the Tuesday of week eighteen.

They were in the yard behind the residence wing, where the stone was cracked and uneven, because Lira said a bout that would be fought on good boards should be practised on bad ones. She had been coming at him for most of an hour in her own fashion, fast and straight, with Wind at her heels, and he had been reading her and slipping her, and his hip had begun to talk.

"I'm trying to work out what he's doing."

"You're trying to work out what he's doing before you fight him, so that you can fight him the way you fought him last time." She stopped a pace off and set her hands on her hips. "He's not the man you beat. Stop fighting that man. Fight the one who turns up."

"I can't fight what I can't read."

"You've been fighting things you can't read since you were fourteen." Lira did not soften it. "You read faster than anybody I've ever met. That's yours, and it's real. But it's a habit as well, and a habit is a thing the other man can learn about you." She came at him again, without warning, and he slipped her, and she stopped. "There. You read me, and you went. Edran's watched you do that more than anyone alive except me. He's had a whole term to think about what to do with it."

Brom was sitting on the low wall with his pocket book open on his knee.

"Count the half-second," said Brom.

They both looked at him.

"Whatever he's bought, he paid a hand of reach for it," said Brom. "Reach is distance. Distance is time. Nobody gives away time for nothing, so he's bought some back somewhere else." He licked his pencil. "Find out where he spends it."

That night Cael wrote the plan for week twenty in the binder, and he wrote it the way he wrote anything that would cost him, with the price beside every line.

*Edran. Eight exchanges on Wray's count. Standings rules.*

*Wind: three bursts free. The fourth bills the hip the next morning. Do not spend the fourth unless the bout is on it and nothing else will do.*

*Read: works. Doesn't tell me what he means. Never has.*

*Ember: no. Not on a training floor, not against a friend, not for an exhibition.*

*Compression: only if he puts a push into me I can't slip.*

*What I don't know: what he bought with the reach.*

The last line sat there looking back at him. After a while he added another below it, smaller.

*Find out in the first exchange, and don't pay for it twice.*

---

## CHAPTER — editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-03.md

# Chapter 3 — Same Terms

The training hall had been swept twice before the first bell, once by the second-years whose turn it was and once by Hobb, who had not been asked.

Cael came in by the side door with his wraps already on and found the gallery full. He had expected that, and it still stopped him in the doorway for a breath. At Greyvane two hundred people was nearly everybody, and nearly everybody was here: on the benches along both long walls, three deep at the rail, sitting on the window ledges with their boots hanging down. The first-years were crammed into the corner by the door, where somebody had told them they could see best and had been wrong. The tout from the river town was moving along the back of the north bench taking wagers in crusts and kitchen duty, quietly, with a great air of not doing anything of the kind.

Three chairs stood at the head of the floor behind a narrow table. Wray had the middle one. Quenna sat on her left with a fresh pad squared in front of her and her pen laid across it, as she had sat at the head of every sitting he had ever given. The right-hand chair was empty, and stayed empty. The terms said two, and Wray did not fill chairs for show.

Hobb stood at the rail by the north mark with his arms folded. He nodded once when Cael came in, and did not do it again.

Lira and Brom and Karis had the end of the front bench. Lira had her elbows on her knees and her eyes on the floor, not on him. Brom had his pocket book out. Karis had nothing in her hands at all, which Cael noticed, and understood, and was grateful for.

Edran was already at his mark.

He stood with his weight low and easy, the off arm carried a little forward, close in along the line of his body, and he was not building anything yet. He looked up as Cael crossed the boards, and there was nothing in his face but attention.

Wray stood.

"Exhibition bout," she said, and her voice went to the back of the hall without her raising it. "Under full sanction, before the faculty, on the record, as both parties asked. Eight exchanges on my count. Standings rules. Each exchange goes to the cleaner touch, or I call it even. Any structure on the body is lawful. Any fragment or declaration the record already carries is lawful. Nothing to the head, nothing to the joints, nothing after my word." She looked at Edran, and then at Cael. "Either of you may stop it. I may stop it. If I stop it, it's stopped, and nobody argues with me afterward in the yard."

"Understood, Instructor," said Edran.

"Understood, Instructor."

Quenna picked up her pen.

Cael went to his mark and set his feet and let his shoulders come down, and ran his prices once more in his head, quickly, the way he checked the knots on his wraps.

*Three bursts free. The fourth bills the hip in the morning. The read works and tells me nothing about what he means. No Ember. Compression only if he puts a push in me I can't get out of.*

*What I don't know: what he bought with the reach.*

He had stopped trying, in the last week, to work it out from the rail. Lira had been right. He had been trying to fight the man he had beaten, and that man was gone. The one at the far mark had spent a whole term taking himself apart and putting himself back together on purpose, an hour at a time, and hating every hour that he needed it, and the only way to find out what he had built was to stand in front of it.

Across the boards Edran rolled his shoulders once and settled. Cael ran the gaze over him from the floor up, the way he would have run it over a stranger in the Ironyard: feet a little wider than they had been in the autumn, the weight even across both, the knees soft. The breath was high and slow. The off arm hung forward and close, the hand open, the forearm turned a little out so that its edge faced the line between them. The lead arm was the same as ever. Every layer agreed with every other, and none of them told him anything he had not seen from the rail.

He had wanted, for a whole week, to come to the mark with an answer already in his pocket. He did not have one. He had a question and a set of prices, and a friend on the front bench who had told him to fight the man who turned up. He found, standing there with two hundred people waiting, that the question was steadier to stand on than an answer would have been. An answer could be wrong. A question could only be asked.

He breathed in and held half of it, and let the rest go.

"First exchange," said Wray. "Begin."

---

Edran did not come forward.

That was the first thing. The old Edran had come off his mark almost at once, faster than the textbook distance, with a shell already bright along his leading forearm, driving it out to be struck. This one came a step and then let Cael come to him, with the structure forming on the lead arm only as Cael closed: a hard pale shell like ice skinning over a trough, there and finished in the space of a blink.

Cael read it. The read laid it on the skin of his forearms a pace and a half out, the weight of the shell and the line it was set along, a fraction before his eyes had it. The gaze had the rest: Edran's breath, held high; his rear foot, flat; the off arm close in, doing nothing. Everything agreed.

He went where the shell would burst. That was the old answer, and it had been a good one. A Glass shell struck hard enough shed the force outward in a spray, and for the length of the spray the arm behind it was bare. He feinted high, and Edran met the feint with the shell, and it burst in a spray of glitter, and Cael was already moving into the space where the bare arm would be.

The arm was not bare.

A second shell had come up on the off arm, which had been carried forward and close in exactly so that it could, and it was already set across the space Cael had moved into. He felt it on the read a breath too late to stop. He turned his shoulder and took the shell on the outside of his forearm, where it burst and stung, and Edran's lead hand came round inside it with the edge of the forearm turned out and laid itself along his ribs. It barely touched, but it was clean.

"Touch," said Wray. "Edran. One."

Somebody at the back of the north bench made a sound that was half a cheer and half surprise.

Cael went back to his mark with his forearm humming and the gaze running over what had happened, looking for the mistake. He could not find one. He had read the shell right, the burst right, and the bare arm right, and moved at the right speed. And the off arm had been there anyway, because it had never been out of place to begin with.

"Second exchange. Begin."

This time he did not go in. He let Edran come to him, and stayed at the edge of the read, and watched.

It took him most of the exchange. Edran came on in short steps, built a shell, let Cael strike at it, shed it, built the next on the off arm, and stepped on. Cael gave ground and slipped and gave ground and let the shells burst on his guard. Twice he saw an opening and moved at it, and both times the off arm's structure was already standing where he arrived. It did not come up to meet him. It was simply there, finished, half a beat before he was.

He tried changing his own timing. He went in early on the third shell, before it burst, to catch the off arm on its way. The off arm was not on its way anywhere. It had never left. He tried going in late, after the spray, when the off arm had closed and might be committed, and found the lead already building again behind it. Edran did not hurry. He did not need to. He gave Cael the floor, a step at a time, and took it back a step at a time, and kept his breath high and slow the whole while.

Every read he had was correct. Every read arrived at the right place half a beat after Edran did.

He understood it in the middle of a slip, with a shell bursting off his shoulder and the spray stinging the side of his neck. Edran had given up reach. With the off arm carried forward and close, it could not go out far to meet anything. But it was always nearly in place already. When a shell burst on the lead, the off arm did not have to come from somewhere; it only had to close the last finger's width. That was what the hand of reach had bought, and it was exactly the half-beat Cael's eyes needed. Edran had not hidden his seam. He had moved his recovery to the place where Cael's reading ended.

A correct read that came too late, Cael thought, was a wrong read, only more expensive.

"Time," said Wray. "Even."

---

He went back to his mark and did not look at the bench, though he could feel Lira not looking at him with the whole side of her face.

Reading the shell would not do it. The shell told him where Edran was, and Edran's whole new stance was built to be somewhere else half a beat later. He had to read something that moved before the shell did.

"Third exchange. Begin."

Edran came on, and Cael let him, and this time he did not watch the lead. He let the lead arm go to the edge of the gaze, where it could be weather. He put his attention on the off arm, carried forward and close, and on the shoulder above it.

The read gave it to him on the second shell. A fraction before the off arm closed its last finger's width, the weight came off the rear foot and into the off shoulder, and the read laid that shift on the skin of his forearms like a hand pressing. It was small, and it was early. It came before the shell burst, before anything Cael's eyes could see, and it told him the off arm was about to move across.

And when it moved across, it left the side it had come from.

He held half a breath and let the Wind take his feet.

The first burst carried him low and left, not toward the shell or the opening behind it, but to the side the off arm was leaving, which for the length of half a beat had nothing on it at all. He came out of the burst inside Edran's guard on the wrong side, the side nobody had been watching, and paid the landing beat locked and still. He laid his palm flat on Edran's breastbone from there, without any weight behind it, because he did not need any.

"Touch," said Wray. "Cael. One each."

The hall's noise came up and broke over the floor. He walked back to his mark and counted. *One.* His hip had said nothing yet.

Edran stood at his own mark a moment longer than he needed to, looking at the place where Cael had come through. Then he looked at Cael, and something in his face sharpened, and Cael knew that Edran had already started working on it.

"Fourth exchange. Begin."

Edran came on as before, and Cael waited for the shift, and the read gave it to him: weight off the rear foot, into the off shoulder, the off arm about to move across. He went with it, left and low, to the side it was leaving.

The off arm did not move.

Edran had put the weight into the shoulder and held it there and built his next shell on the lead instead, the old way, and Cael came out of his slip into exactly the place the lead shell was waiting. He got his forearm up. The shell burst on it with a crack like a branch breaking under snow, and the off arm, which had been loaded and still, came across now, late on purpose, and laid its edge along his flank.

"Touch. Edran. Two to one."

He went back to his mark with both forearms aching and his breath short, and he made himself think instead of feeling.

Edran had learned the read in one exchange. He had felt where Cael came through and given him the same shift with nothing behind it, and Cael had believed it, because it was true weight, honestly placed. The read never lied about weight. It only ever lied about what weight was for.

*Read the shift. Then wait for the arm.* It would cost him time. It would cost him the early arrival, which was the whole of the advantage. But a shift with nothing after it was a feint, and a feint could only be known by what came next.

"Fifth exchange. Begin."

It went long. Edran pressed, and Cael gave ground slipping, and twice the shift came and twice he waited, and twice it was nothing. On the third the off arm moved. It moved fast, but Cael was already turning. The framework took him round on the second figure, the turn Wray had broken his count on all term and that he had spent the whole recess mending on frozen stone, and it brought his weight over on time, not a tenth late. He came out of the pivot square to Edran's open side with his feet under him.

The second burst was short and almost easy. He had his palm on Edran's breastbone and the landing beat paid before the off arm had finished crossing.

"Touch. Cael. Two each."

Behind the narrow table, Wray made a mark on her own slate. She did not look up. Cael understood, from the fact that she did not look up, that she had seen exactly which figure the pivot had come from.

---

The sixth exchange was the worst one, and he would think about it for a long time afterward.

Neither of them waited. Edran came off his mark with a shell already up, faster than he had come all morning, and Cael understood that Edran meant to take the exchange before the read could settle. He came in close and stayed close, building and shedding on both arms so fast that the read was a smear of weight and the gaze could barely keep up. Cael slipped once, and again, and on the third shell he felt the shift and saw the off arm commit, and he went.

The third burst took him in.

But Edran had committed honestly this time, and he was quick. As Cael came out of the burst on the open side, Edran was already turning into him, shedding the lead shell in a spray that went into Cael's face and building the next on the off arm as it came round. Cael's palm went to the breastbone. Edran's structure went to Cael's chest. The spray from the burst and the touch of the palm arrived together, so close together that Cael himself could not have said which was first.

Both of them stopped.

The hall stopped with them. In the quiet Cael could hear his own breath, and Edran's, and somewhere at the back a first-year saying "Who?" in an anxious whisper and being hushed.

Wray did not speak at once. She sat behind the narrow table with her hands flat on it and looked at the two of them standing locked together in the middle of the boards, Cael's palm on Edran's breastbone and Edran's shell standing an inch from Cael's ribs, unburst.

"The shell didn't land," said Wray. "The palm did. Touch. Cael. Three to two."

There was a noise from the north bench that did not sound like a cheer.

Cael stepped back. He looked at the shell on Edran's off arm, still whole, and at the inch of air between it and where his ribs had been. If Wray had called it the other way he could not honestly have said she was wrong. Edran's structure had been coming. It might have arrived a hair after his palm or a hair before. The call was hers to make, and she had made it, and it was defensible. That did not make it the only defensible call.

Edran let the shell go. It broke outward in pale glitter, and he went back to his mark without looking at the table.

Quenna wrote something on her pad and did not look up.

He had spent three bursts, and he had two exchanges left.

His hip had begun to speak at the end of the sixth, a warmth low on the leading side, not yet the line. He knew what the line would be if he asked it for a fourth. On stone, it came due in the morning. On these boards, with Wray's sweeping in them and a little give underfoot, he did not know, and he had never paid it in front of two hundred people.

*Do not spend the fourth unless the bout is on it and nothing else will do.*

"Seventh exchange. Begin."

He tried to win it without the Wind.

He went to the read and the gaze and the framework, slipping on his own feet and taking shells on his forearms. He waited for the shift, and on the second true one he went in on foot, without a burst, using the pivot, aiming to arrive on the open side by turning instead of flying. It was slower. It was half a beat slower, which was exactly the half-beat Edran had bought with his reach. He arrived on the open side, and Edran's third structure of the sequence was standing there on the off arm, whole, thick and clean from the elbow to the wrist, without any thin place or cold sliver on its edge.

Cael looked at it for the space of a single heartbeat, with real admiration. He could not help it. It was a beautiful piece of work, a whole term of somebody's life, finished.

Then its edge was along his ribs.

"Touch. Edran. Three each."

The north bench came to its feet. The tout had stopped taking wagers and was standing on the bench with his mouth open.

Cael went back to his mark. His forearms ached from wrist to elbow. His breath was short and his hip was warm. There was one exchange left, and he had won three of them and been given one of those three, and Edran had won three outright. He stood at the mark and looked at the man at the other end of the boards. He knew he could not get to the open side in time on his feet, and that he had one burst left before the bill came due. He knew too that he was going to spend it.

He did not pretend otherwise to himself. He had written the rule. He was breaking it in full knowledge of what it would cost, and he would write that down too, in the morning, when the cost had arrived.

"Eighth exchange," said Wray. "Last. Begin."

Edran came on. He knew it as well as Cael did. He came on low and patient and let the shells come up and shed, lead and off, lead and off, and waited for the fourth burst to come. If Cael had been in his place, he would have been doing exactly the same.

Cael waited for a true shift.

The first was a feint. He felt it, and held, and the off arm stayed. The second was a feint too, and he held again. On the third, the weight went into the off shoulder and the arm began to move, and Cael held half a breath and went.

He knew at once it was wrong. The fourth burst always cost something before it cost anything else, and this time it cost him distance. He had placed it to bring him out on the open side a hand from Edran's breastbone, with his palm already there along the line of travel. It brought him out half an inch short. The landing beat locked on him, longer than it ever ran, the half-breath stretched into most of a whole one. He could not change direction and could not reach. He could only go on along the line he had chosen, with his arm bent where it should have been straight.

His palm touched the breastbone anyway. It was not much of a touch. It had none of his weight in it, only the end of the burst's travel arriving a moment after he did.

Edran's off arm finished crossing and found nothing in front of it.

"Touch," said Wray. "Cael. Exchange and bout, four to three."

The noise in the hall came up all at once, and it did not sound like the noise after the formal yard.

That one had been the noise of people who had seen something happen that they had not expected. This one was the noise of people who had watched two fighters do a thing all the way through, and knew that it had been close, and could not agree how close. Half the north bench was shouting about the sixth exchange. The tout had climbed down from the bench and was sitting on it with his head in his hands, which Cael took to mean he had laid his crusts badly.

Cael stood in the middle of the boards and let it go past him. His hip had stopped being warm and begun to be something else, a narrow brightness low on the leading side that was not yet the line and would be. He knew he would walk on it tonight and pay for it tomorrow. It did not matter yet.

Edran was in front of him.

He had let the last of his structures go, and his forearms were bare and red where the shells had sat all morning. He was breathing hard and trying not to show it. He looked at Cael for a long moment, the way he had looked at Cael through the whole bout, without anything in his face but attention.

Then he put out his hand.

That was new. He had never offered it before, not in the formal yard and not at the rail, not even when he had turned his back to say he was glad the reading had held. Cael looked at the hand for a moment before he took it. It was a hard, dry grip, and it did not let go quickly.

"Third one someday."

Cael saw it in Edran's shoulders before he heard it in the words. It was not a courtesy. It was not a challenge or a complaint. It was the next line of a ledger they were both going to keep.

"Someday," said Cael.

Edran let go and went to his corner without another word, and Hobb came off the rail and handed him a towel, and Edran took it without looking.

Wray was standing behind the table. She looked down the length of the floor at Cael, and he waited for her to say something about the sixth, or the fourth burst, or the landing that had run long in front of the whole school. She did not.

"Pivot," said Wray. "On time. Second figure." She sat down. "That's all."

He bowed to the table. Quenna's pen was still moving. She did not look up, and he did not expect her to.

---

He walked to supper on it, and walked up the stair on it, and by the time he sat down at the desk by the window the narrow brightness had become the line. It ran from the crest of the leading hip down the inside of the thigh to the inner knee, bright and exact, like a seam somebody had stitched into him with hot wire. He sat with it and did not reach for the binder at once.

He had won. He knew that. Wray had called it, and Quenna had written it, and two hundred people had seen it, and nobody could take it off the record now. He also knew how close it had been. He had won one of the eight exchanges on a call that could have gone the other way. He had won another on a burst he had promised himself he would not spend, which had landed half an inch short of where he put it. And he had lost a third to a man who had learned his best read in one exchange and handed it back to him empty.

There were two ways to write it down. He could feel both of them sitting in the pen.

The first way was easy and true in every word. *Edran, exhibition, week twenty. Won four to three in the eighth exchange. Found the recovery in the off arm in the third; corrected in the fifth; pivot on time.* Every word of that was so, and it would read beautifully a year from now, and it would be a lie by omission. A lie by omission was still a lie, and he had built his whole life since Denvash on not telling any.

He dipped the pen and wrote the other one.

*Edran. Week twenty. Full sanction, Wray presiding, Quenna second seat. Eight exchanges.*

*Margin: one exchange. Possibly none. The sixth was a palm against an unburst shell arriving together. Wray called it for me. I can't say she was wrong. I can't say she was right either. If it goes the other way I lose.*

*What he built: he gave up a hand of reach to carry the off arm forward and close, so that his recovery is already in place when the lead bursts. Every read I had arrived correct and half a beat late. A correct read that comes too late is a wrong read that costs more.*

*Correction (found in the third, broken in the fourth, mended in the fifth): don't read the shell, read the off arm's shift. Then wait to see whether the arm follows, because he'll feed me the shift empty. That costs the time I was trying to save. It's still the only read that worked.*

*Wind: four bursts in eight exchanges. One over my ceiling. I spent the fourth knowing what it would cost, because nothing else was going to get there. It landed half an inch short and the landing beat ran long, longer than I've ever felt it. Hip: the line, crest to inner knee, tonight already, on boards. Expect three days.*

*Without the fourth there's no eighth exchange, so I'd pay it again. That isn't the same as saying it was cheap. I went over my own ceiling to win a bout I'd promised myself I'd win inside it. Against somebody better, one burst over wouldn't have been enough, and I'd have been two over, or three, and still lost.*

He read it back once. It was not flattering. He found that he did not mind.

Lira knocked and came in without waiting, as she did. She had a cloth folded in her hand, wet from the pump and cold enough that he could feel it across the room. She held it out. When he took it, she leaned over his shoulder and read the page in the binder without asking, all the way down to the last line, and straightened up.

She did not say anything about the sixth exchange or the fourth burst. She did not say *well fought*. She put her hand on top of his head for a moment, briefly and rather hard, as if she were testing whether it was attached.

"Three days," she said. "I'll hold you to it. No floor, no yard, no bursts. If I catch you on the stair taking it two at a time, I'll make it four."

"Three days."

"And next time," said Lira, from the door, "don't make Wray choose."

She shut it behind her. He sat with the cold cloth on his hip and the binder open in front of him, and thought that she was right, and that it was the best thing anybody had said to him all day.

---

## CHAPTER — editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-04.md

# Chapter 4 — Soon

Quenna found him at the pump on the Thursday of week twenty-one, at the end of the afternoon, when the yard was emptying and the light had gone long and yellow across the stones.

His hip had given him back on the fourth morning, as he had written that it would. He had kept Lira's three days to the hour. He had not taken a stair two at a time, though he had wanted to on the second evening, badly, and Lira had been on the landing above with her arms folded as if she had known. Now he was standing at the pump with the handle under one hand, not pumping, only feeling the iron cold under his palm and the old ease back in his leading side. He was thinking about nothing in particular, which was rare enough that he noticed it.

She came across the yard from the old grey building with her hands in her coat and stood beside him. She did not look at him. She looked across the yard toward the road-house end of the academy, where the stone was oldest.

"Told you it would be soon."

He waited for the rest.

There was no rest. She stood a moment longer with her hands in her coat, and then she took one hand out and laid two fingers on his sleeve, briefly, just above the wrist. Then she put the hand back in her pocket and went away across the yard toward the old building, not quickly, and went in.

He stood at the pump with the handle under his palm and did not move for a long time.

He knew what she had not said. He knew it as well as if she had sat down on the edge of the trough and said all of it in her driest voice, one sentence after another, the way she read a record into the roll. She had made a bet once, sitting down on a bench beside a boy nobody could classify, with her name in her pocket. It had been the largest bet of her life, and she had lost a good deal on it before it paid. She had stood behind him through a winter of sittings and a hearing, in front of a magistrate and the Compact's own officer, in a chair one row back at his own request. And now a house four times the size of this one was taking him on a documented basis, in law. For two years Quenna had looked for exactly that, a ground for him to stand on that was not borrowed, and had not found it. It had been sitting the whole time in Prynn's fifth case, waiting for Karis and her ruler.

She had not said any of that. She had not said that she was proud of him, or that she would miss him, or that she wished it had been her. He knew all three were true. He knew she would never say a word of any of them, not to him, not to anybody, because Quenna did not put things into words that she could not afterward stand behind on a record. And she could not stand behind *I wish it had been me*. It was too much like asking for something.

So she had said one sentence instead, the one that held all of it and gave none of it away. Standing at the pump, he weighed the sentence she had spent against the paragraph she had kept back, and knew which of the two had come dearer.

He did not go after her. He knew that going after her would make her say something, and then the sentence would be smaller.

He thought instead of a question she had asked him months ago, in the week after the match with Karis. She had asked it not for the file but for herself, and he had written it in the binder the same night because he had not known the answer. Whether he could *not* do it. Whether, in front of people who wished him harm, with everything lined up the way it had lined up once in a formal yard, he could hold still and keep his hands to himself.

He still did not know. He had not been tested. She had not asked again, and he did not think she would, and he was not going to go across the yard and offer her an answer he did not have.

He let go of the pump handle. The iron had warmed under his hand. He wiped his palm on his coat and went in to supper.

---

Naveth's last notice went up on the board in the main hall on the Monday of week twenty-two, below the posting for the end-of-term sittings and above a sheet about the kitchen rota.

It was one line long, in the clerk's small even hand. It said that the enrollment of the candidate on the observer track concluded at the end of the present term by transfer to a chartered institution, at the candidate's request, as entered by the provost. It cited nothing. It explained nothing. Karis read it three times and pronounced it perfect.

"Not one interesting word," she said. "He's done it again. That paper is going to sit in a drawer for a hundred years without anybody ever wanting to read it."

"Is that good?"

"It's the best thing that can happen to a paper." She copied it into her narrow brown notebook, all of it, the date included. "The interesting ones get read by people with pens."

The last days went quickly and slowly at once.

He wrote to Hesk on the Tuesday night. It was a short letter, shorter than the last, because he had learned that Hesk read the short ones more than once. He said where they were going, and why, and that there was a registrar at the other end who had asked how to spell Hesk's name and had got it in capitals. He said the four of them were well. He did not say anything about the bout. He thought about it, and found that what he wanted to write was *I won by one exchange and maybe none*, and he could not see a way to put that to Hesk that would not sound like asking to be told it was all right. He left it out. Hesk would hear it from somebody, or he would not, and either way it would keep.

He had not heard from Vell.

The letter had gone down the hill at the end of the winter term, in his own hand, with the ruling copied out under it word for word. It had been five months. He had stopped listening for the coach somewhere around week six, and had started again around week fifteen, and had made himself stop a second time. Vell had never in her life answered anything quickly. She read a letter the way she read a ledger, every line in order, and then she put it somewhere and let it settle before she touched it again. He knew that. It did not make the silence lighter. It only told him what shape it was.

Wray found him on the defensive floor on the Wednesday morning, where he had gone out of habit to run the framework one last time on her stone.

She did not come over at once. She stood by the rail with her slate under her arm and watched him run it, four passes, at panel speed. She broke the count on the third pass, as she always had, and said nothing about the pivot, because there was nothing to say. When he finished she came down off the rail and stood a pace away from him with her hands behind her back.

"Your ceiling," she said. "Three free on stone. You told my sittings that, in those words, every time I asked."

"Yes, Instructor."

"You measured it on my stone." Wray looked down at the floor between them, old grey flags worn smooth in the middle by forty years of feet. "My stone is the hardest floor in the district. I chose it because it doesn't lie to anybody. But it isn't the only floor in the world, and a number you measured on one floor is a fact about that floor as much as it's a fact about you." She looked up. "Where you're going they'll have boards, and halls built for the purpose, and floors I've never stood on. Measure again. Don't carry my number into their hall and call it yours."

He thought about it. He thought about the line from crest to knee after the fourth burst on Wray's swept boards, and how it had come early, and how he had not known whether it would.

"I'll measure again," he said.

"Good." She turned to go, and then turned back. "And find somebody there who'll break your count on the third pass. Everybody gets lazy on the third pass. You more than most, because you're good at the first two."

She went back up the rail and out by the side door, and that was all the goodbye there was. He stood on her stone a while longer and found that it had been exactly enough.

He packed on the Wednesday. It did not take long, because there was not much. Two shirts and his good coat. The wraps. The observation notebook, with Greyvane's section full and closed and a blank page already headed for the next place. Hesk's letter, in the inside pocket where Brom's page had once been. The binder last of all, a third full now, with Lira's slip in the back pocket behind Karis's consent and the term's count on the last written page.

Karis packed in an hour, and most of the hour was books. She had a coat with a deep inside pocket that buttoned, and she had worn it every day since the autumn. On the Wednesday night, while Cael watched from the door of her room, she checked the pocket the way he checked the knots on his wraps, without seeming to think about it. She put two fingers in, felt the folded paper there, took them out, and did up the button. She did not look at him. He did not ask. There was a copy of something in that pocket that both of them had written out by hand from a register that had not come back up the hill, and neither of them had understood it, and they had agreed without saying so that it would stay buttoned until they did.

"Ready?" said Karis.

"Nearly."

"Liar," said Karis, quite kindly, and put out her lamp.

---

Prynn came to the gate on the Thursday morning of week twenty-two.

Nobody had ever seen Prynn at the gate. Cael was not sure anybody had seen her farther from her own door than the long table at the end of her own room, and he had sat at that table for the best part of a year. She came down the cart road from the old building in the grey light before the first bell, in a black coat that looked older than the academy, walking slowly and without a stick. She had something under her arm.

The four of them were at the gate with their bags. The gate clerk was in the lodge with her list, and the cart that would take their bags down to the stage-house was standing in the road with the carter on the box, and two of the five watchers were where they always were at that hour, one at the gate post and the one who only worked mornings by the wall of the stable. Lira saw Prynn first. She stopped talking in the middle of a sentence and put her hand on Cael's arm.

Prynn did not greet any of them. She came up to Cael and held out what she had under her arm.

It was a sheaf of paper, thick as a thumb, sewn along one edge with linen thread and bound between two boards covered in grey cloth. He took it. It was heavier than it looked. He opened it at the first page and saw her hand, small and upright and perfectly even, in a black ink that had not faded at all.

It was an index. It listed the citations of registry law in Prynn's archive, every one she held, and each entry carried three cross-references. One was by subject, one by date of enactment, and one by the clause each entry amended or was amended by. Every entry ended with the name of the compilation the text could be found in and the shelf-mark where that compilation actually stood, in this archive, on this hill. He turned the pages. There were more than fifty of them. Some of the entries had small marks beside them, a tick or a cross or a short line.

"The ticks are sound," said Prynn. "The crosses are struck. The lines are the ones nobody has looked at in so long that I couldn't tell you, and neither could anybody else, and you'll want to find out before you lean on them." She looked at the sheaf in his hands and not at him. "The next place you go will have a bigger archive than mine and a worse one. Big archives always are. They take on young people to catalogue and then they make the good ones into keepers of keys, and the cataloguing stops, and nobody notices for twenty years. You'll find what you need in a place like that only if you know what it's called before you go in. This is what it's called."

"Prynn," he said. "This must have taken—"

"It took the winter." She did not let him finish. "I don't sleep well in the winter. It was something to do."

He did not believe her for one instant, and she knew he did not, and she did not care.

"Thank you," he said.

"Thank me by opening it." She had already put her back to him. She had gone three steps when she stopped, without turning round, and spoke over her shoulder in the same dry level voice she used for shelf-marks.

"The shelf gap is still there. It'll be there when you're forty."

Then she walked on up the cart road, slowly, without a stick, and did not look back.

He stood at the gate with the sheaf in his hands and watched her go. He had thought, for the whole of the term, that the space on the third shelf of the second-to-last case was a thing Prynn could not bear to close. He saw now that he had had it the wrong way round. She was not failing to close it. She was keeping it open. She would go on keeping it open every morning she unlocked her door, for the rest of her life, as the one entry in her archive that could not be answered with another entry. It would wait there for whoever came back to ask about it. She had just told him who she expected that to be, and roughly when.

Karis had come up at his shoulder and was looking down at the open sheaf with an expression he had last seen on her face in the intake room at Greyvane, when she first heard the word *archive*.

"Shelf-marks," she said, under her breath. "She's given you the shelf-marks. Every one." She did not reach for it. She kept her hands at her sides, very deliberately. "Do you know what that's worth? In a strange archive, the first month goes on finding where things are. She's given you the first month."

"You can borrow it."

"I'll copy it," said Karis. "On the road. Every evening. You can read it while I copy." She looked up the cart road after Prynn's black coat. "I'm not borrowing the only one of those that exists. She'd know. From four days off, she'd know."

Brom had picked up all four bags while they were talking and was standing by the cart holding them, two in each hand, as if they weighed nothing, waiting for somebody to tell him where to put them.

He put the index in his bag, on top of the binder, and buttoned the flap.

"Well," said Lira, very quietly, beside him.

"Yes," he said.

The gate clerk leaned out of the lodge window with her list. "Four of you going out," she said. "I've had you down as going out since the second bell yesterday. Are you going out, or are you going to stand in my gateway all morning admiring the archivist?"

They went out.

---

Four days of road lay between Greyvane and the river, and on every mile of it somebody in grey kept them company.

Cael counted them from the first morning, because counting was what he did with his hands when there was nothing else for them, and because a thing counted was a thing he could stop being afraid of. He did not have to look hard. Nobody was hiding.

*Day one. Two. Both mounted, both in grey, a quarter-mile back on the road from the foot of the hill, keeping the same distance whether we walk or stop. Changed at the second waystation for two more, also grey, also mounted. Neither pair spoke to the other. They nodded.*

It was good walking country after the first morning. The cart road off the hill joined the high road at the stage-house, and the high road ran south and east along the top of a long ridge with farms falling away on both sides, sheepfolds and stone walls and the brown of turned fields. The four of them walked it in the order they always walked a road without ever having agreed to: Brom in front because his legs were longest, Karis beside Cael in the middle because she liked to talk while she walked, and Lira a few paces back. She said it was so that she could see everything. Cael thought it was so that she could see him.

They let the cart go on ahead with the bags and walked behind it. Nobody suggested riding. They had walked into Greyvane, and it seemed right to walk out.

Karis walked beside him with her narrow brown notebook open in one hand and a pencil in the other. She wrote while she walked, which Cael had never been able to do without falling over. At the noon stop they laid the two notebooks side by side on the wall of a sheepfold and compared, line against line, as they had compared the consolidations.

"Same," said Karis. "Except you've got the second pair changing horses and I haven't."

"You were looking at the first pair."

"I was looking at the first pair because the first pair was looking at us." She made a small correction in her own book. "Yours is better. I'll take it."

*Day two. Three. The two mounted from yesterday's change, and one on the coach with us from the second stage, a grey coat with a satchel on his knees. He took the seat with the widest view of the four of us, and kept it. He read a book. He didn't turn a page in an hour.*

That afternoon, rattling along in the coach with the grey coat across from them pretending to read, Brom leaned across and spoke to Cael very low.

"He's not even trying."

"No."

"Last year they tried." Brom looked out of the window at the two riders keeping their quarter-mile. "Last year it was one man at the edge of a crowd who you couldn't be sure of. Now it's three, and one of them is sitting on my bag." He considered this. "Why'd they stop trying?"

Cael had been thinking about it since the foot of the hill, and he gave Brom the answer he had come to.

"If a man hides while he watches you, he wants to learn something," he said. "He wants you to forget he's there, so that you'll do whatever you'd have done anyway, and he can write it down. A watcher who doesn't hide wants the opposite." He kept his voice under the noise of the wheels. "He wants you to know. He wants it to change what you do. He's not collecting anything. He's trying to make something happen in you."

Brom thought about that for most of a mile.

The coach rocked down off the ridge into a valley of orchards, and the grey coat across from them turned a page at last, and Lira, who had been asleep against the window, opened one eye and looked at him and closed it again.

"Is it working?"

"I don't know yet," said Cael honestly. "Ask me at the other end."

---

*Day three. Two. New faces, both mounted, took over at the river stage before we were up. One of them has a horse with a white sock on the near hind. The coach man from yesterday got off at the river stage and didn't get back on.*

*Day four. Two. One is the white sock from day three. The other is the coach man from day two, riding now, on a brown horse that isn't his. He doesn't look at us. He looked at us for a whole day yesterday from three feet away and now he doesn't.*

The fourth night they slept a half-day short of the river, at an inn with a low room under the eaves, and there he laid the observation notebook flat under the lamp. He added it up the way he would have added up a column in Vell's ledger.

*Four days. Distinct faces: seven. Distinct changes of watch: five. Never fewer than two on us at any time. Three at junctions and at the inn stops, where the roads split and they couldn't be sure which way we'd take. Nobody spoke to us. Nobody needed to.*

*What this costs them: two men and a horse-change every eighty miles, for as long as I'm worth watching. That isn't a whim. It's a budget. Somebody signs for it, and keeps on signing.*

*Their side of it: I know they're there, and knowing is supposed to bend me. My side: seven faces, five rotations, a white sock on the near hind, and a fair idea of what the Compact thinks I'm worth per mile.*

He sat back and looked at it.

Brom had come in behind him with two mugs of something hot from the kitchen. He set one down at Cael's elbow, read the page over his shoulder without asking, and sat down on the end of the bed, which complained.

"Does it bother you?" said Brom.

"Less than it should." Cael turned the mug on the table and thought about it again, because Brom's questions deserved a second try. "They're in plain sight. I've got every one of them on a page, and a fair idea of the bill, and I could tell you now where they'll stand at breakfast. That's not what frightens me." He looked at the page. "What would frighten me is a morning with nobody on the road behind us, and no reason I could find for it."

Brom drank some of his mug.

"That'd mean they stopped."

"Or that they got better."

Brom looked at him over the rim of the mug for a while. Then he nodded slowly, once, as if he had been handed a weight and found it heavier than it looked, and drank the rest without saying anything.

Karis put her head round the door a little later with her narrow brown notebook in her hand.

"Five changes," she said. "I make it five. You?"

"Five. Seven faces."

"Seven." She came in and sat on the other end of the bed from Brom, who made room. "Then here's what I keep coming back to. They aren't hiding. That's a decision. And they aren't stopping, either, not even now that the ruling's public and there's nothing left to find. That's a second decision, and it costs money every month, and somebody has to put their name to it every month." She closed the notebook on her pencil. "The first one I understand. That second one has a name signed under it somewhere, every month. I'd like to read it."

"So would I."

"Then that's two of us who'll be counting at the other end." She got up. "Goodnight. Don't sit up writing."

She went out. Lira was asleep already in the next room, and now Karis was writing by her own lamp next door; he could hear the pencil through the wall. Cael turned back a few pages in the binder to the last entry he had made at Greyvane. It was the term's count, written at the long table on the last night, before the gate and Prynn and the index. Under it there was half a page left. He looked at it for a while, and then wrote two lines at the foot of it, in the square hand.

*Greyvane. Twenty-two weeks. A word found and not killed. A bout won by one exchange, perhaps none. A sentence from Quenna and a book from Prynn, and both of them meant more than they said.*

*Done. Tomorrow, the river.*

---

## CHAPTER — editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-05.md

# Chapter 5 — The First True Sentence

The toll board at the near end of the bridge had nine kinds of traveller painted on it, and Cael was still reading the sixth when Brom put eight coppers on the ledge.

*Persons on foot, two coppers. Persons with a pack beast, five. Handcart, laden, four. Handcart, empty, two.* Then a line for drovers, charged by the head, and a line for wagons, charged by the axle. Below those were three he had to read twice. *Persons bearing goods for the customs house, no charge on the house's tally. Persons of the river guilds, by their lodge's arrangement. Academy enrollees in the first week of any term, on presentation of the gate list, one copper.* The last had been painted in later than the rest, in a whiter white, and its price had been painted over at least twice. Every line on that board, Cael thought, was the healed-over place where somebody had won an argument with the toll keeper's grandfather.

"One each for enrollees," said Karis. "It's on the board."

"With the list." Brom collected four wooden tallies from the toll keeper and handed them round. "The list is at the top of that hill. We're at the bottom of it."

"I could have paid my own."

"Next time," said Brom, and walked on.

There was never a next time. Somewhere between Ardenmere and here it had become simply one of the facts of travelling with Brom, like his long stride and the way he ducked under doorframes he would have cleared. About once a week there was a toll or a ferry or a loaf at a stall, and Brom's hand was on the ledge first, and nobody was allowed to mention it afterward. Cael put his tally in his pocket and followed him out onto the bridge.

It was old stone and very long: five spans on four piers, each pier with a pointed cutwater shouldering into the current. Halfway across, at the crown of the middle span, Lira stopped at the parapet without saying why. The other three stopped with her, because that was what they did.

Below them the Ost went by wide and slow and brown. A barge was working up against it, sitting low with sacks under a tarred cloth, two men leaning on the long sweeps and a boy at the tiller, the three of them bent as though they were shoving the whole river uphill. Another came down the other way easily, sweeps shipped, and its master stood on the cabin roof and put a horn to his mouth and blew three short notes. From somewhere on the near bank a second horn answered with two.

"What's that?" said Lira.

"He's taking the middle arch," said Karis, "and somebody on the bank is telling him it's clear. I think. Give me a week and I'll have every call they've got."

Along the near bank the town went on as far as he could see each way, warehouses shouldering down to the water. He counted six long sheds with their doors open on the river and a seventh going up in raw yellow timber. A public wharf had a wooden crane on it with four men walking its wheel, and below that a second, smaller wharf stood behind a fence with a company's mark on its gate. Between the bridge foot and the public wharf, across the only street that ran down to the water, a building of good grey stone sat with its own steps and its own dock and a flag over its door. Nothing could come off a boat and into Ostrand, Cael saw, without passing under that flag.

"Customs," said Karis, watching where he looked. "Above the bridge the river's too shallow for anything big. So everything coming down out of the hills gets broken out of carts here and loaded onto boats, and everything coming up gets the reverse. Whoever stands at the place where one kind of carrying turns into the other takes a share of both." She leaned her back on the parapet and recited it as if she were reading it off a page in her head. "About twenty thousand people. Fourteen guild lodges. And two registry offices on Weighbridge Street, a hundred yards apart from each other. One is a district office and one is a stations office."

"Why have two?" said Brom.

"I don't know yet." Karis said it the way she said things she meant to fix. "But Lira's flag is going into a registry somewhere, and when it comes out it'll come out of one of those two doors. I want to know which door before it does."

Lira did not look round, but Cael saw her shoulders change.

He was looking past all of it, across the water, at the far bluff.

Halcenvane stood along its top. The first thing he noticed was that nothing in it had been inherited. Every school he had ever seen had been made out of something older: a road-house, a granary, a lord's barn with a floor laid in it. This one had been built to be what it was, all at once, by somebody who had drawn it first. The roofs all ran at the same pitch. The stone was the same pale stone from end to end. Along the bluff's north lip ran three long halls with their windows set high under the eaves, all on the side the sun came up. One heavy-roofed building had walls thick enough to carry a great weight of something, and he thought it must be books. A separate block sat behind a low wall of its own. And in the very centre, where everything else on the bluff seemed to lean toward it, there was a great hollow ringed on three sides with tiered stone seats.

He found he could not take his eyes off the hollow. Whatever it was for, the whole place had been arranged around it.

He had been keeping the count of days since the gate at Greyvane, every evening, in the margin of the observation notebook. It was five months and nine days, tonight, since a magistrate had stood up in a crowded hall and read him a ruling. He was fifteen years old. He had added the months up twice on the road against Hesk's last letter, because he no longer trusted any number that only he was keeping.

At the near end of the bridge, by the toll house, the two grey coats who had ridden the last stage behind them had got down from their horses and were not crossing. One handed his reins to the other and walked off along the bank toward the ferry landing.

"Changing at the river," said Lira, very low.

"Yes."

"I'll have them all by the end of the week," said Lira. "Come on. I want to be at that gate before somebody decides to shut it."

---

The road up the far side climbed the bluff in two long switchbacks, and it had passing bays.

Cael noticed them at the first bend, because passing bays were a thing nobody bothered with on a road meant for people walking. These were wide enough to pull a loaded wagon into while another went past it, paved at the edge, with a stone post at each end for the wheels to keep off. The road had a gutter on its uphill side and a culvert at each turn. Somebody had spent money on this road, and kept on spending it, which meant that the money went up and down this hill every week in wagons and somebody needed it to arrive.

At the foot of the road, where it left the river street, two men in grey coats were sitting on a low wall outside a chandler's. They were not the two from the bridge. They did not look up as the four of them turned onto the hill, and Cael added them to the count and did not look back.

"Two at the ferry," said Lira, behind him. "Two here."

"Changing every four hours, if it's the same as the road."

"We'll see." Lira hitched her pack higher. "I've got all season."

Halfway up they stepped into a passing bay to let a wagon come down, four casks roped in its bed and the brake shoe screaming on the wheel. The carter lifted two fingers off the reins at them and went past. Brom lifted two fingers back. When Cael raised his eyebrows at him, Brom shrugged.

"Carters," he said. "You always answer a carter. They remember who didn't, and they remember it on narrow roads."

The wall at the top came up no higher than Cael's chest, pale and new and clean-jointed. Nobody could have defended it, and nobody had meant to. It only marked a line on the ground and said *here*. The gate stood open with both leaves folded back. Beside it was a lodge with a glass window, and in the window was a man with a ledger, and in front of the window was a queue: three carts, a woman with two boys and a trunk, a porter with a handbarrow, and a young man in a good coat arguing about something with nobody in particular.

The man in the lodge was dealing with all of it at once and not enjoying any of it. They joined the end of the queue and waited for a quarter of an hour, and when they reached the window he did not look up.

"Names."

"Cael. Lira. Brom. Karis Dellenmoor."

He found the page without hunting for it. He read it off fast and flat, in the voice of a man who had read sixty names since the first bell and could hear the next cart's wheels already.

"Dellenmoor. Standard. Research standing pending; the library committee writes to you. Brom. Standard. Ninth day, first bell, east hall; the Blade cohort wants a look at you. Lira. Transfer, re-certification. Copper bracket. You register for the ladder by the fourteenth or you start the season on the bottom rung and climb from there." He licked a finger and turned the page. "Cael. Assay-provision enrollment. Straight to the registrar. Assessment wing, ground floor, the door with the brass plate. He's cleared the whole morning, which he does not do, so go now and look at the view later."

Then he did look up, once, at all four of them, with the face of a man who had a cart horse breathing on the back of his neck.

"Rooms, second quadrangle. The porter's got your keys. The assessment wing has a wall of its own; you'll know it. Don't cross the Crown yard when there's a bout on; they'll shout at you, and I'll be glad they did." He was already reaching for the next carter's slip. "Welcome to Halcenvane. You there, with the casks."

They went through the gate.

Cael had braced himself for it since the bridge. The only other gate in his life that had kept a list with his name on it had nearly stopped him dead on its threshold with something too large to have a name. He waited for that to happen again, and it did not. He walked through between the folded leaves and out the other side, and the only thing he felt was a kind of quiet click, like a latch going home in a door that had always been meant to close. Four people, four lines in a stranger's book, and four roads already decided in ink by people who had never seen their faces. Nobody at this gate had needed to argue for any of it. The arguing had been done somewhere else, months ago, on paper, and here was only the result.

It was very restful. He was not at all sure that it ought to be.

---

There were twelve desks in the intake hall and four of them were working.

The other eight stood under linen dust sheets down the far end of the room, in two rows, square and pale, like a second set of applicants who had come early and been told to wait. Cael stood in the line for the fourth desk and looked at them for a long time. The hall ran a hundred feet under a coffered ceiling, lit down its whole west wall by tall windows, on an oak floor laid for this room and no other. Somebody had sized it for an academy three times the one that was using it this morning. Somebody had looked at Halcenvane as it was, and built a room for Halcenvane as it meant to become, and paid for it, and then sat down to wait for the school to grow into its own furniture.

Greyvane had built for the people it had. He had loved Greyvane for that. He did not know yet what to feel about a place that built for people it had not met.

He counted arrivals, because it was something to do with his eyes. In the hour and a half he sat waiting, the double doors let in thirty-one people. His whole first year at Greyvane, all told, had come to forty-three.

Brom was done before Cael had counted ten. He came back across the oak with a tag on a cord round his neck, turning it in his fingers and looking injured.

"Nine minutes," he said. "I timed it by the bell. She looked at my papers the way my aunt looks at eggs. Held each one up, decided it wasn't cracked, put it in the basket." He sat on the bench beside Cael. "I've been the easy one twice now. You'd think I'd get used to it."

"East hall on the ninth," said Cael.

"Blade cohort. Everybody I've asked says the same thing about it. Go and stand in front of that lot for an hour, they say, if you'd like to learn what slow means." Brom tucked the tag inside his shirt. "So that's my ninth. A lesson in slow. I can't wait."

Karis came back from her desk with three forms and a letter, and her face held the particular delight she kept for documents that had clauses.

"There's a schedule of terms for the registry-law holdings," she said. "It's eleven clauses long. The ninth says what happens when the library committee and a reader disagree about access, and it gives the reader a right of appeal to the chancellor in writing." She sat down on Cael's other side and laid the forms on her knees as if they might take fright. "Greyvane had Prynn. Prynn was worth more than eleven clauses and a chancellor. But if Prynn said no, there was nowhere to go but the stove."

Lira was at the third desk for most of an hour.

Cael could not hear all of it from the bench, but he could see it, and he could read Lira standing at an official table with her arms folded better than he could read most people's faces. Her clerk was a grey-haired woman with spectacles on a cord. Lira's file had come up from Greyvane ahead of her: the old Fenmark record, the acknowledgment of standing she had signed before any of them knew her, and, pinned at the back, the sheet Greyvane had given her. It was the flag asking the registry to look at her tier again, under Greyvane's seal, the provost's whole two terms of watching folded into one line.

The clerk read the flag through. She gave it a single nod, wrote two words beside it, and set it on a pile by itself.

"That goes on," Cael heard her say. "To the registry's review queue. It isn't ours to decide. It's theirs."

Lira said something he could not hear.

"It moves," said the clerk. "Not fast. I've sent three on from this desk since the spring before last and had three answers back, so it isn't a hole in the floor. Until it comes back, you're placed by your formal standing." She turned a page. "Copper."

He watched Lira's jaw set.

They went on for a while longer, the clerk explaining and Lira not arguing, which was worse. *Only an Arbiter evaluation can change the tier*, the clerk said at one point, clearly enough for Cael to hear it across the room, and *I don't like the pace of it any more than you do, dear*, and *register early; the people who matter read the boards.* And then Lira unfolded her arms, put both hands flat on the edge of the desk, and said something that made the clerk look up at her properly for the first time.

The clerk thought about it. Then she dipped her pen and wrote a line at the foot of Lira's sheet, and turned it round so Lira could read it, and Lira read it and nodded, and signed under it.

She came back across the hall with a face like a shut door.

"What did you ask her for?" said Karis.

"The date it goes out," said Lira. "The day the flag leaves this building for the registry. I made her write it on my sheet, with her initials." She sat down hard on the bench. "If I'm going to wait for a queue, I'm going to know what day I started waiting."

Then the clerk at the fourth desk called Cael's name.

---

Registrar Bracken had a pen behind his ear and another in his hand and four stacks of paper on a desk with nothing else on it.

The stacks stood a hand apart, squared to the edge, each with a narrow slip on top and a number on the slip in a small upright hand. Cael knew that hand from twenty-two letters. Bracken himself was a spare man in a grey coat, greying at the temples, with a clerk's stoop he corrected whenever he caught himself in it, so that every few minutes he seemed to grow an inch. He did not offer his hand. He pointed at the chair.

"You've read the letters," he said. "All of them?"

"All of them."

"Then we start at the fourth stack and work backward." Bracken sat. "Everybody wants to start at the fourth stack. It's the one with your name on it. So we'll look at it first, and you will not sign it, and then we'll go back through the other three until you understand why it stands up. Then you'll sign it. Not before."

He turned the fourth stack round and slid its top sheet across the desk. Cael began to reach for it, and Bracken put one finger on the corner.

"Read it," he said. "Don't touch the pen."

*Enrollment record. Halcenvane Academy, chartered.*

*Category: assay-provision practitioner: evaluation by demonstration per charter clause, standing basis.*

*Entry requirement: satisfied by demonstration before the assessment office, this term.*

*Ongoing requirement: full evaluation by demonstration before the panel of the assessment office, each semester, without exception. Compact observation rights attach.*

He read it once. Bracken took it back and set it face down on the desk.

"Now," said Bracken, "why should anybody believe a word of it?"

In the first stack lay the clause, copied out of three founding-era compilations, every copy certified, every one tagged with where its book stood and which clerk had sworn to it. Bracken read out each mark. Cael, because the index was in his bag, took it out and found each one in Prynn's small upright hand and read her cross-reference back. Two of the compilations were ones Karis had worked from. The third Greyvane had never owned.

On the second certified copy, Cael stopped.

"The date on the clerk's certificate," he said. "It's a year after the date in the compilation's own colophon. But the shelf-mark is for the first printing."

Bracken took the sheet back and looked at it for a long moment. Then he took the pen from behind his ear and made a small mark in the margin.

"The copying clerk's slip," he said. "He took the shelf-mark off the first printing and the certificate off the second, because they stand side by side. The text's the same in both. I checked that myself. But the sheet says two things at once, and anybody who wanted to could pull on that thread." He set the sheet aside on a pile of its own. "It'll be redone this week. That's what I asked you here for. Thank you."

Bracken called the second stack the negative case. In it were the three lists of repeals, and the consolidations, and a table ruled in his own hand, list against list and page against page, with the clause missing from every column. Under the table he had written two sentences. The first said what the absence showed. The second said, just as plainly, what it did not show, and why nobody should read it as showing that.

"Most people would leave the second sentence out," said Bracken. "It looks like weakness. It's the strongest line in the file." He tapped it. "Never let a document," he said, "claim more than it proves. If it claims one line more, that one line is where it gets attacked. Then people stop reading, and the whole file goes into the fire with its true lines and its false one together."

"Karis says nearly that."

"She's seventeen, your Dellenmoor. I asked her outright, after my forty-one came back answered better than I'd asked them. I didn't believe the first reply." Bracken squared the second stack. "Third."

The third stack was the hearing.

It held the whole transcript, both sittings, every exhibit, the deposition and the ruling, bound in grey boards and tabbed down the fore-edge in four colours. It was the copy of somebody who meant to work from it, not keep it nice. On top lay the ruling, with a pencil line under two of its sentences.

"Your floor and your difficulty, both in one binding," said Bracken. "Your hearing settled that what people do and what is written are two different things, and the written one wins. That's your floor. This clause is written, and all that ever happened to it was people stopping. And it puts your name at the top of a public record that anybody between here and the coast can send for at six silver marks. That's permanent. Both halves of it are permanent." He looked at Cael over the boards. "You knew that when you argued it, I suppose."

"Yes."

Bracken studied him for a moment longer than he needed to.

"Yes," he said. "I think you probably did. Fourth again."

He turned the enrollment record face up and slid it back.

Cael read it a second time, and then a third, and knew he was doing it, and knew why. He had done it once before. That had been at a narrow table at Greyvane, with Quenna on one side of him and a cold cup at his elbow, over a form that said *unclassified observer*. He had read it three times then not to understand it, but to make himself agree to sign a thing that was not him. The form had held. Every word on it had been true. Not one of them had been about him, and everybody at that table had known it.

This one was about him. It did not say what he could do. It was careful not to, and he could see now that the not-saying was what made it strong. It said only how he and the academy stood to each other. Here was a person whose capability no registry line could give, so it would be shown, in a room, before instruments, every semester, for as long as he was here. Nothing in it was hopeful. Nothing in it was kind. Every word of it was so.

"Well?" said Bracken.

"It's the first one that's true."

Bracken was quiet for the space of a breath. Then he picked up the pen and laid it beside the sheet.

"Two signatures," he said. "The first is for the standing. The second is for the obligation, and I want it on its own line, in its own stroke. A man who signs both with one movement can say later he only read the half he liked." He watched Cael's hand. "Somebody will come up that road one day to read this with a grudge. Inside two years, I'd guess. Keep that in the same pocket as the rest."

Cael signed twice, in the square hand he kept for the binder. He watched the second signature go dull as the ink dried. Something rose in his chest and his eyes stung, all at once and without asking him. He turned his head and looked very hard at the window, which showed a slice of pale wall and a gutter.

Bracken had got up and gone into the cabinet room behind the desk. Cael heard a drawer open and stay open for a long time.

When Bracken came back he had a tag on a cord and a folder, and he wrote on the folder's cover before he put it away: the date, and under it, *94 min.*

"I'd allowed two hours," he said, as if he were confessing to a fault. "The assessment office will set the day for your entry demonstration. Magister Gault sits that panel. His face gives nothing away. That isn't a judgment of you; it's his face. Whatever you show him that day is your baseline, and the baseline doesn't move. Every semester's evaluation is measured against it, as long as you're here." He held out the tag. "Choose with that in mind."

Cael took it. At the door he turned.

"Twenty-two letters," he said. "Why so many?"

Bracken had already sat down to the next thing. He did not look up at once.

"Twelve years I've kept these rolls," he said. "In twelve years, every record I've filed has been true because nobody ever had cause to look at it closely. Yours had to be true *because* I did the work properly. One day somebody will be paid to hunt for the flaw in it." He looked up. "Do you know how long I've waited for a file like that?"

---

Lira was on the wall before any of them, alone, with her elbows on the coping and the river below.

It ran along the edge of the bluff behind the second quadrangle, chest-high, the same pale stone as everything else, and beyond it the ground fell away in rough terraces to the water. Ostrand lay across the river with the late light going gold along its roofs. Down on the wharves somebody was lighting lamps, one after another, and the lamps ran out along the waterfront like a line of stitches.

Cael came and leaned beside her and did not say anything. After a while she spoke without turning her head.

"Did it say what you are?"

"It said how I stand." He thought about it. "Which is nearer than anything's ever said before."

"Good," said Lira. She meant it. He could hear that she meant it, and he could hear the other thing underneath, and she did not hide either.

Brom and Karis came across the grass from the refectory with half a loaf each and a twist of salt and a pot of something that might have been butter. Brom put the pot on the wall. Karis took a key on a string out of her collar and laid it beside the pot as if it were the more important of the two, which to her it was.

"Carrel eleven," she said. "Third floor of the law range. A door that shuts. A key. If I leave a book open on the desk tonight, it'll be open at the same page in the morning, with nobody's elbow in it."

"Is eleven a good number?" said Brom.

"It's a number." Karis put the key back inside her collar. "Greyvane had a table. I loved that table. But you can't lock a table."

Lira tore off a piece of Brom's bread without asking.

"Copper bracket," she said, to the river.

Nobody answered. Nobody needed to.

"Two terms of putting Iron-equivalents on the floor. An academy's seal on a flag. A queue that's answered that woman three times since the spring before last." She ate the bread. "And they read the number on my certificate, and they stop reading. They always have."

"The flag transfers," said Brom, carefully.

"The flag transfers into a queue." Lira turned round and put her back to the wall. "I'm not sulking. Somebody write that down, because I'll want it later. I got through a gate today Greyvane couldn't have opened for me in ten years of trying, and I know who paid for it. But I'm going to be extremely tiresome about that bracket for one season. Then I'm going to take its crown off it. And then I'm going to stand in front of that queue with the crown in one hand and the date the clerk wrote in the other, and we'll see how fast it moves."

Brom considered this with great seriousness.

"So it's a plan," he said.

"That's a grudge with a schedule." Lira took the last of Brom's bread. "Same thing, if you keep the schedule."

Cael took out the binder. He set it open on the coping next to the butter, and nobody looked away, and nobody looked over his shoulder either. That was how it had been at this end of every day for a long time now.

He wrote it plainly.

*Enrolled.*

*Second time the word's been used about me. First time it's been accurate.*

He sat looking at it while the light went red on the river. Then:

*Karis found it and tried for eleven weeks to kill it, and it wouldn't die. Bracken checked it from the other end because he wanted to, not because anyone asked. A counsel I'll never meet put a seal on it, and Naveth read the seal twice and was glad for me in a way that cost him. Every form anybody ever got me through before was true in its words and false about me. This one is true about me. It took a lot of people to make one sentence.*

*I intend to be worth the sentence.*

He left a line and went on, because an entry that only counted what he had got was half an entry.

*What it costs: a panel every semester, a room, a full evaluation each time, and a chair in that room for the Compact whenever it wants one. Whatever I show Gault first becomes the baseline, and the baseline doesn't move. For as long as I'm here. Nobody has ever told me a price in advance before, in writing, and let me read it twice before I signed.*

*I'd have paid more.*

He closed the binder. Down on the water the last lamp on the last wharf came alight. Lira bumped her shoulder against his, once, the old knock, and stayed leaning there. Brom passed the butter. Karis, beside them, was already writing her own day into her own book, small and fast. In the middle of the bluff behind them, in the hollow of stone seats he had seen from the bridge, nobody had lit a lamp at all.

---

## CHAPTER — editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-06.md

# Chapter 6 — Four Hundred and Twelve

On the first full morning he was out of the quadrangle before the fifth bell had finished, with the observation notebook buttoned inside his coat and a single question to carry. He carried one into every new place. It kept his eyes honest.

The question was not *what is this place*. Karis already had that out of the prospectus, and she would have the rest of it out of the library inside a week. His was older and sharper, and he had brought it down from Greyvane in the same pocket as the notebook. *Why did they take me?* Every school he had stood in told one story across a desk and another in its stone. The desk story was for visitors. The stone story was in which roof got mended first and which teacher got the good hall, in what was weighed every week and what was left to the damp. Anybody willing to walk slowly could read it.

Greyvane's stone had told him everything in three days. He allowed this place four, and he was wrong.

He walked the north edge first, where the three long halls stood with their high windows facing the morning. The largest had six floors marked out on its boards, and somebody was already on one of them at that hour, alone, running a drill he did not know. The second hall was smaller and its floors were timber, not stone, and when he stood in its doorway and shifted his weight he felt the boards answer him very slightly, a give and a return, as though the floor had a breath of its own. He wrote that down and underlined it and did not yet know why. The third hall was low and set apart behind the assessment wing. Its door was locked from the outside, with the key on a nail beside it, which was a thing he had never seen before and could not think of a reason for.

He did not try the key. He wrote that down too.

The assessment wing had its own wall and its own gate, and through an open door on the ground floor he counted pieces of apparatus standing along a wall under cloths. There were eleven. He could guess the purpose of four. The library was the heavy-roofed building he had picked out from the bridge, and an accession board inside its door claimed forty-one thousand volumes in brass numerals. Karis, when he told her that night, said that accession boards counted everything a library had ever been given, including the things it had since burned for being damp, and that she would believe thirty-five.

The residence was three quadrangles, and it was the residence that gave him the first true line.

The first quadrangle was the oldest and had the best rooms, with deep windows and doors that shut properly. The third was newest and largest, built in a hurry, and its stair rang under every step. Theirs, the second, sat between. On the second morning he watched the porters carrying trunks across from the gate, and he noticed which trunks went where. It was not by tier, or not only by tier. A Copper girl whose tag carried a small painted mark went to the first quadrangle. Two Iron boys with plain tags and quiet faces went to the third. He asked the porter, idly, what the painted mark was.

"Seeded last season," said the porter. "Top four in her bracket. They get first pick of rooms."

"And the Iron boys?"

"Didn't enter," said the porter, and shrugged. "Never do, those two. Good lads."

Cael wrote that down in the notebook, and then sat on the steps of the second quadrangle for a long time looking at it.

Lira found him there a little after the second bell. She had been up before him, which was usual, and she had already walked the whole of the Crown yard's rim once, slowly, and stood a long while at the registrar's table at its side, where the ladder list lay open for names. She had not written hers.

"The clerk at intake said early," he said.

"The clerk at intake said by the fourteenth." She sat down beside him on the step and stretched her legs out and looked at the toes of her boots. "Early's for people who want to be looked at. I want to do the looking first. Every name that goes on that list before mine is somebody I can watch walk up to that table and choose to be counted." She shrugged. "I'll put mine on when I've seen enough of them. Not before."

"That's a schedule."

"It's a grudge," said Lira. "I told you. Same thing, if you keep it." She looked at the notebook on his knee. "What have you got?"

He showed her the line about the rooms. She read it twice, and then gave it back to him without saying anything. That was how he knew it was true.

*Rooms go to the ladder. Not to tier, not to need. To whoever looks as if they might bring something home for the school.*

Greyvane had sorted by need, every time. It had been the whole of what Greyvane was, and the whole of what was wrong with it, too, because need was the one thing nobody could ever pay a bill with. Halcenvane sorted by something he did not yet have a word for. It had to do with how near a person stood to what the place wanted to become.

---

On the third morning, at breakfast, he found an entry in the notebook that was not true.

It was in his own hand, from the evening before, in the middle of a page of others like it: *Current Path, tall, dark braid, third year by the tag. Favours the right leg coming down stairs. Old injury, healed short.* It was a good entry. He remembered making it. He remembered the braid, and the stair outside the north hall, and the small hitch in the right leg on the second step down.

The tall girl with the dark braid came down the refectory steps past his table two at a time, light and quick, with nothing wrong with either leg at all.

He watched her all the way to the hatch and back. He watched her sit. When she got up again to fetch water he watched her go down the two steps to the pump trough, and then come back up them, and there was no hitch.

He went back through the notebook, very slowly.

He had sixty-three entries from three days. He went through every one of them, asking of each the same question: *Did I see this, or did I fill it in?* For most he could not tell. That was the thing that turned him cold, sitting at the long refectory table with his porridge going hard in the bowl. It was not that some of the entries were wrong. Any method made mistakes. It was that he could not tell the wrong ones from the right ones by looking at them. They were all in the same hand, in the same confident sentences, with the same small details. The limp had been real. He was nearly sure the limp had been real. It had belonged to somebody else, a boy perhaps, somewhere on the same stair, and at some point between the stair and the notebook his mind had taken the limp off the boy and put it on the girl. It had done it so smoothly that he had never felt the seam.

He went to the north hall after breakfast to find out where the seam was.

A Force Path cohort was on the largest floor, twenty-two students and four instructors, running a rotation he had not learned. He sat on the gallery bench and did the thing he had been doing for three days without thinking about it. He ran the gaze across the floor, all four layers at once, rhythm and weight and breath and position, building each student as a live thing in his head and keeping them all moving.

Then he did what he should have done on the first day. He chose one student, a stocky boy at the north end, and shut his eyes, and said under his breath what the boy was doing. He opened them and checked. He was right. He did it with two students, then four, then six. He was right every time.

At nine he was right about eight of them.

At eleven he was right about seven, and wrong about the other four in ways that sounded exactly like being right. He had a girl stepping off on her left foot who was stepping off on her right. He had a boy holding his breath at the top of a push who was breathing out. He had an instructor standing at the west end who was standing at the east. The wrong answers did not come to him blurred or doubtful. They came complete, with all their details, as clear as the true ones. When he passed a dozen, the gaze did not lose its grip and go vague. It went on handing him pictures, as sure and detailed as ever, with nothing behind them.

He sat on the bench with his hands between his knees and made himself put a price on it, honestly, there and then, before he could start to make it smaller.

*North hall, third day. I wasn't tired. I'd slept and eaten. This is a limit, not a lapse. Past nine people, somewhere short of twelve, the gaze stops seeing and starts supplying. It doesn't fade to warn me. It keeps its sure voice. It hands me a whole person with every detail in place, and some of the details were never there. I've been wrong that way for three days and never once felt it, because at Greyvane nine was a crowded floor.*

*Bill: three days of pages. Mending them won't do. I can't tell the lines I saw from the lines the gaze made up, so every line goes.*

He took the notebook back to his room at noon and cut the three days' pages out along the binding with Karis's penknife, borrowed. He folded them small and took them down to the stove in the quadrangle's common room and fed them in, one at a time, and watched each one go brown at the corners and curl and catch. Sixty-three entries. Some of them had been the best observation he had ever done. He did not know which.

It took most of the afternoon to stop feeling sick about it.

---

He rebuilt it that evening in carrel eleven, because Karis had a door that shut and a lamp that did not smoke, and because he did not want to do it alone.

He told her all of it first. She listened without interrupting, with her chin on her hand. When he had finished she was quiet for a while.

"You're trying to count every sheep on the hill," she said.

"What?"

"There's a way they survey a hill farm for the tithe. They don't count every sheep. Nobody could. They count the folds. They take one fold in every so many and count every animal in it properly, and they write down which fold and which day. For the rest they write how many folds, and roughly how big, and leave it." She turned her pen in her fingers. "The tithe man isn't lying about the sheep he didn't count. He's writing down that he didn't count them, and how he guessed. It's the same number of sheep either way. It's a different kind of honest."

He turned it over while the lamp burned down a finger's width. Then he took a clean page and built it.

It came in three tiers. He named them as he wrote them, because a thing he could not name he could not keep.

*Standing.* These were the people he would carry at full depth all the time, kept live and updated every day. Eight of them at the most, and never nine, because nine was where the failing started and he meant to keep one place clear between himself and the edge.

*Sampled.* These were people he would look at properly, but only at set times, one session in four, with the date written beside every entry, so that he would always know how old a thing was before he leaned on it.

*Structural.* This was everybody else. He would not hold them as people at all. He would hold them the way the tithe man held the folds he never opened: as a cohort and a drill and a habit, as how a group moved and what its instructors kept correcting, as where its middle sat. He would write a whole hall down in four lines and know he was doing it.

His pen did not want to write the third tier. To put it down was to admit, on paper, that the hill held four hundred people he had already chosen not to see properly. He made the pen do it. Then he boxed the reason, so that he would have to look at it every time he opened the page.

*This tier is here because the other way invents people. Better a fold I know I never opened than a sheep I wrote down that was never in it. The tithe man isn't lazy. He's telling you which numbers he counted.*

"Who goes in the eight?" said Karis.

He had been afraid she would ask that. He had not let himself think about it yet, and now that she had asked it, he saw why.

At Greyvane there had been no choosing. Nine people had been the most on any floor, and he had held every one. Here he would have to choose. Choosing meant putting down on paper, privately, his own ranking of who on this bluff was going to matter. It meant deciding which nine-tenths of the people around him would be weather.

"It's a ladder," he said slowly. "It's my own private ladder. I'm going to rank people by whether they'll matter, before I know them, and then I'll only look properly at the ones I've already decided matter."

"Yes," said Karis. "Everybody does that. You're only the first person I've met who's going to write the list down."

He wrote three names and stopped. *Bracken.* *The Blade instructor, Rooke, whom Brom stands in front of on the ninth.* *The porter, who knows where everybody sleeps and why.* He left five lines empty under them.

*Eight is a ceiling, not a target. Leave room. Whoever earns a line, earns it on the page, with a date.*

The five blank lines sat under the three names. They made him less uneasy than he would have expected. A list with room in it was a list that could still be wrong out loud.

Karis leaned over and read the three names upside down.

"The porter," she said.

"He knows where everybody sleeps, and why. He knows which trunks go to which quadrangle before the trunks do." Cael considered it. "He might be the best-informed man on this bluff, and nobody's ever going to ask him anything."

"You're going to ask him things."

"I'm going to listen to him tell me things he thinks are dull. It's not the same." He capped his pen. "People tell you the most when they think it doesn't matter."

Karis looked at him for a moment with an odd expression, half amused and half something else.

"You know that's what you do too," she said. "Every time you write in that book in front of us. You think it's the dull parts we don't notice."

That one he could not answer. He put the notebook away, and she let him, and they sat a while longer in the carrel with the lamp between them and the door shut on the whole of the law range, and neither of them minded the quiet.

---

He kept the Crown yard back until the sixth morning on purpose. He wanted the whole bluff understood first, so that when he finally stood in the middle he would know what it was the middle of.

He went at dawn, when it was empty, and he did not go to the seats. He went down onto the floor.

It was dressed stone, sixty feet by forty, laid so close that he could not get a fingernail into the joints. And it was sunk. The whole floor lay four feet below the ground around it, with three short flights of steps going down into it. The tiered seats did not rise from the floor's edge the way the benches at Greyvane had risen. They rose from the ground's edge, from above. He stood in the middle of the stone floor and turned slowly round, and on three sides the seats went up and back from a point above his head, row on row, pale and empty, and every one of them was looking down at him.

That had been meant. Somebody had dug a hole and put the fighting at the bottom of it, so that a thousand people could sit above and look down on two. He stood at the bottom of it in the grey light and felt it on the back of his neck, the way he had felt the gaze of a full gallery at Greyvane, though there was nobody here at all.

He counted the seats. It took a while. He made it a little over eleven hundred, which was more people than Halcenvane had enrolled. So the yard was not for the students alone. It was for the town, and for visitors, and for whoever the academy wanted to show something to.

The fourth side of the yard, the open side, had a raised stand for faculty, a long table with a registrar's chair, and four boards painted black and mounted on the wall behind them.

He was reading the boards when the porter came down the steps with a pail of chalk and a rag and said, "You'll get shouted at."

"There's no bout on."

"There'll be one at the fourth bell. You'll get shouted at in advance." The porter was a heavy, slow-moving man with a groundskeeper's arms and a habit of speaking to the ground in front of him rather than to anybody's face. He set the pail down and began wiping last season's headers off the first board with long even strokes. "You're the assay one."

"Yes."

"Thought so. You read things." He wiped. "Go on, then. Read 'em. I'll tell you what you get wrong."

He read them and wrote them down as he went, in the shorthand he kept for things he would need again.

*Boards, four. One: the year's afternoons, both terms, with the windows for challenges marked in red between. Two: Copper. Three: Iron. Last season's order still up on both and fading, each line a name, a rank, fights, wins, a place. Four: headed Silver. Half the length of the others.*

"Silver's short," said Cael.

"Silver's everybody above Iron." The porter did not stop wiping. "Bronze and Silver both. Fifty-one Bronze on the hill this year and fourteen Silver. Not enough of either for a bracket of its own, so they all go in one, and it takes the name of the top of it."

"Three brackets for five tiers."

"Five tiers if you count the Gold, and she doesn't count." The porter jerked his head toward the north halls. "One Gold on the bluff. She's not on any board. Finished her coursework two years back and stayed on, under some fellowship the chancellor's office drew up. Doesn't fight on the ladder. Nobody within two tiers of her to fight." He started on the Copper board. "Two hundred and nine Copper. Hundred and thirty-eight Iron. The fifty-one and the fourteen. That's your whole school."

He added it while the porter was still talking. The Copper and the Iron together came to three hundred and forty-seven, and the sixty-five on the short board brought it to four hundred and twelve.

Until that moment he had not known how many people the bluff held. Standing there he saw that he still did not. What the porter had given him was a tally of papers. The Gold fellow was not in it, because the brackets had run out of room above her. And he was not in it either. The brackets had never had a column for him at all. There were two people on this bluff that the boards could not describe, one at the top and one off the side, and neither of them appeared on any wall.

*The bracket lines look like they tell you who's good,* he wrote, on the steps afterward. *What they tell you is how many there are. Where the count runs thin, the school gives up dividing and calls whatever's left Silver.*

"How does it work?" he asked the porter. "The ladder."

The porter told him, wiping, as a man tells a thing he has told so often he could tell it asleep. Cael put it straight into shorthand.

*Ladder. Four fights a session afternoon; the assessment office pairs them. In between, you may call out anybody above you in your own column, four names up at most. Beat him and his place is yours, and everybody between slips one. Lose, and the board stays as it was.*

"Nothing?"

"Nothing on the board. Costs you plenty in your face." The porter shook out his rag. "But sit idle four sessions running and you slide a place on your own. So you can't sit on a good standing and hope. You've got to keep putting your name up. That's what drives the whole thing. Not the winning. The having to keep at it."

*Sit out four sessions running and you slip one anyway. Year's end: the best four in each column are seeded to the semifinals, which run in the second term's closing weeks. The final comes on the year's last day of competition. Faculty sit as the panel. The registrar keeps the record.*

"And the winner?" said Cael.

The porter stopped wiping.

"The winner gets the crown," he said. "There's three. Copper, Iron, Silver. They live in a case in the chancellor's hall and I polish them, so I know." He started wiping again, slower. "The Copper's the eldest by a long way. They've been riveting new brass on under the old for a hundred and thirty-one years, to make room for the names. Two years now it's been Fiske's. Force Path, Copper Rank Eight. Put her in the Iron column tomorrow and she'd walk off with that one as well."

"Then why isn't she in it?"

"Her certificate says Copper." The porter wrote *COPPER* in big square capitals across the top of the clean board. "The board doesn't care what you can do, lad. It reads what's on your certificate and puts you where that says. It's not unkind. It's a board."

---

He took Brom down to Ostrand on the ninth evening, because Brom needed new wraps before his first cohort hour. He had been assessed that morning in the east hall and placed in the Blade instructor's cohort, and the first hour was set for the following week. About the assessment he had said only that the cohort was fast, and Cael had decided not to ask him for more.

They crossed the bridge on foot. Brom paid. On the river street, in the long light, they walked past what Cael had come down to see. There was not one practitioners' outfitter in Ostrand but a whole row of them, five within a hundred yards of the bridge foot, every one with wraps and soft-soled shoes and padded jackets in its window. The prices were lower by a third than anything in Ardenmere. Five shops in one street selling the same thing meant a great deal of it was being bought, every season, by somebody. Along the river road the boarding houses had painted boards out by their doors. *Term lets. Rooms by the term. Academy families welcome.* The paint had been laid on so often that the letters had gone soft and thick at their edges.

Brom did not say much on the way down. He walked a little faster than usual, and twice Cael caught him turning his right hand over and looking at the back of it, the way he looked at a joint after a fall to see whether it had swollen. He had not fallen. Nothing on him was swollen. Cael watched him do it the second time and did not ask, because Brom had a face on him that was not ready yet, and Brom always came out with things in his own time and never before.

"The whole town leans uphill," said Brom, reading the boards.

"A hundred and forty years of it."

They went into the third outfitter's, because Brom liked the look of the woman behind the counter, who measured his hands without being asked and told him he had been buying his wraps a finger too short for years. Brom accepted this calmly, as he accepted most truths about himself. While she cut, Cael leaned on the counter and asked her, idly, how much the ladder paid.

She looked at him over her shears as if he had asked her the price of the river.

"Pays?" she said. "Nobody's paid on that hill. It's an academy."

Brom stopped turning the cut cloth over in his hands.

They walked back to the bridge in silence. Halfway across, at the crown of the middle span, Brom stopped at the parapet where Lira had stopped on the first day, and looked up at the bluff.

"At the Ironyard a man fought for eight marks," said Brom slowly. "Nine a month in rent, eight a bout. You could see why he got up off the floor. He'd eat if he did." He turned the new wraps over. "Nobody up there's going hungry. So what are they fighting for?"

Cael had been working at it since the outfitter's counter, and he had it.

"Standings. That's the currency."

Brom looked at him.

"It changes into everything else," said Cael. "Rooms. You saw the trunks. The instructor's eye, who gets a word after the drill and who doesn't. Which cohort you're put in, and who you train beside. And Karis says the standings feed something bigger, something the academy wants more than any of that." He looked up at the dark hollow of the Crown yard, just visible over the wall at the top of the bluff. "Nobody hands you a purse at the end, because the prize is how they treat you for as long as you're here. Every bout is paid for in where they let you stand next year."

Brom was quiet for a long time.

"I liked the eight marks better," he said at last. "You knew what you'd got."

Two nights later, on the eleventh day, Cael wrote it down at the desk by his window, with the second quadrangle dark below and the lamps of Ostrand across the river.

*Eleven days. Why they took me: not finished. But I know what they spend on, and that's half of it.*

*They spend on the yard, and the boards, and the rooms that go to whoever's seeded. They spend on a walled wing full of instruments under cloths. Nobody's paid in coin here. They're paid in standing, and standing pays in everything.*

*A circuit that pays coin has to tell you the price before the bout. You know what you're fighting for, and so does everybody watching. A ladder that pays in standing never has to say. It just moves you up or down a wall, and lets you work out afterward what it cost.*

*I'm not on the wall. The Gold fellow isn't either. Two of us outside the count, for opposite reasons.*

The last line looked back at him. He added one more under it, smaller.

*Brom: Rooke took him into the Blade cohort on the ninth. First hour next week. He's gone quiet the way he goes quiet before a weight he hasn't lifted yet. Leave it for him.*

---

## CHAPTER — editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-07.md

# Chapter 7 — Ledgers

The woman at the refectory hatch had been giving him extra since the first night.

She was big and tired and kind, with flour worked into the creases of her knuckles, and she ladled stew as if every bowl were a small argument she meant to win. He had thanked her every time. On the thirteenth evening she leaned toward him over the hatch with the ladle still in her hand and lowered her voice, so that the line behind him would not hear.

"I heard about your people," she said. "How they gave you over to the registry when you were small, once they saw what you were. Signed you away and never wrote you a line." Her eyes had filled. "And look at you, love. Look how you've come on, for all that. I wanted you to know somebody here thinks so."

He stood at the hatch with the bowl in both hands and found that for a moment he could not say anything.

He was thinking of a workbench in Denvash under a smoking stove, and a square careful hand teaching his own to make letters. He was thinking of a letter folded once, with no initial at its foot, because the man who wrote it had got up from the table the instant he was done.

"That isn't true," he said. "But thank you. It was kindly meant."

She patted the back of his hand across the hatch, plainly not believing a word, and put another ladle in his bowl.

He did not eat much of it. He sat at the end of the long table with the bowl going cold and the observation notebook open beside it, at the last page, where for twelve days he had been keeping a list he had never meant to keep.

It had started on the third day with a second-year in the corridor outside the north hall who had stopped talking as Cael passed and then started again a little louder. It had gone on from there. People here did not wonder what he was, the way they had at Greyvane. They told each other. Each of them had a version, and each version was finished, and no two of them were the same.

The others had been bringing him more without being asked.

"Second-year on the east floor," Brom had said, on the ninth night. "Says you're a Gold walking about in a Copper's coat, sent down to see how the brackets are run. He liked it a great deal. I didn't spoil it for him."

Karis had heard one on the third floor of the library, through the wall of the next carrel: that the Compact was paying his fees to keep him somewhere it could see him. She had thought it rather clever.

Lira had brought three in one evening and refused to say where she had got any of them. He had been put out of two academies before Greyvane. He was twelve and small for it. He was twenty and lying.

Now there were eleven. He went through them at the end of the long table while the refectory emptied round him, and he set them in order by how many mouths each had passed through on its way to him.

Nearest the record stood one sentence: *made his argument in public, and the law turned out to say what he said it said.* That was so. As far as he could tell, it was held by the registrar and the chancellor and perhaps two others, all of whom had read the transcript.

One mouth further out it became *beat the Compact in court.* That had not happened. A panel had found that the Compact's own schedule did not name him, and had dismissed a challenge for that reason and no other. But the two were close enough that putting the one right would only make him look as if he wanted to be thanked for being modest. He let it stand and hated letting it.

Past that they went wide. He had no Path at all. He had a secret one. He was a Gold in disguise. He was the Compact's kept boy. He had fought a Warden bare-handed and put him on the floor, which had never happened anywhere, and which he could not trace to anything at all. He had been put out of two academies. He was twelve. He was twenty.

He wrote the eleventh at the very bottom, under all the rest, alone.

*That my people gave me up. The worst of the eleven, because it's the only one anybody has ever passed on to me with a sorry face.*

Then he sat a while longer, and then he wrote the part that mattered.

*At Greyvane the question of what I was had no answer, and so it stayed open. That was hard to live in, but an open door can still be walked through later, by somebody carrying proof.*

*Here every door is already shut, and every one on the wrong room. Eleven wrong rooms. People here meet me already knowing, and knowing is the end of looking.*

*It's easier to fill a bare room than to clear out a full one. People don't give up furniture they chose themselves. They'll defend the chair before they'll let you show them the floor.*

*So this is worse than Greyvane, in a way I didn't see coming. Get it on the page tonight, before I find reasons it isn't.*

---

The first open session of the faculty fell on the fourteenth day, in the lecture theater. By charter, any enrollee who cared to climb the steps could sit in the back five rows and hear the school argue with itself.

Lira had found out about it and saved him a place with her boot. He had expected the back rows to be empty. Instead there were sixty-odd students in them, some writing, most just watching, like people at the rail of a ring before the bout. Down on the floor of the theater the faculty sat round a long table: forty of them, with Chancellor Withrow at the centre and a clerk at her elbow keeping the minute book. The theater could hold two hundred and forty. It felt full.

Withrow did not once glance up at the back rows. She did not send anybody out, and she did not drop her voice when the talk turned to things a school might want kept in the family. After a while Cael stopped waiting for her to. It was not carelessness. She meant the back rows to hear. A school whose quarrels were held where its students could listen could not pretend afterward that the quarrels had never happened. She had chosen that, some years ago, and she went on choosing it every term.

The item came up a little after the half-hour. The clerk read its title off the agenda. *Enrollments resting on a provision: report of the registrar.* Bracken stood, spoke for less time than it took the sand in the clerk's glass to run, and sat. Nobody asked him anything.

Then Instructor Rooke spoke from his chair.

Cael had already put him on the list. He had stood nine minutes at the door of the east hall on the fourteenth morning and watched Rooke take the Blade cohort through a drill. He had hardly looked at the cohort. He had watched Rooke not moving. The man was Silver-tier and past fifty, narrow and grey, and he held still between corrections like somebody who had learned long ago exactly what each movement cost him and would not pay for one he did not need.

"The provision may be sound. The attention it buys us is not. I want it minuted that I said so before the inspection notice arrives, not after."

Nobody spoke. It went on long enough that Cael heard the clerk's pen stop.

Withrow lifted her eyes from the agenda for the first time that afternoon.

"Minuted."

The pen started again. Withrow turned a page of her agenda. "Next, the bursar, on the glazing in the north hall." The bursar stood up and began to talk about cracked panes and the cost of lead.

That was the whole of it.

Cael ran the weight layer on Rooke for as long as the session lasted. Rooke's hands lay flat on the table. His breathing was low and even. He did not look at Withrow after she spoke, or at Bracken, or up at the back rows where the subject of his objection was sitting. There was nothing in him to find. There was no heat in him and no dislike, and no grudge that Cael could trace in any layer.

Afterward, on the way out, Cael stopped at the board by the theater door where the clerk had already pinned the session's minute. He read down to the item.

*Instr. Rooke: the provision may be sound; objects to the attention it draws; asks that his objection stand dated before any notice of inspection. Chancellor: so minuted.*

He stood reading it for longer than it needed.

He had come to this bluff braced for the kind of opposition that showed in a body. He knew how to fight the kind that set its shoulders before it spoke and came at him across a floor. This was not that kind. Rooke had written down a price in public, before it fell due, so that nobody could ever say afterward that it had not been named. Cael had done the same thing with his own life every night for two years, in a binder, with nobody watching.

He could not argue with it. He could not get past it on a floor. It was simply there, dated, on a board, in a clerk's hand, and it was very possibly right.

"Well?" said Lira, beside him.

"He thinks I'll cost them more than I bring."

"Will you?"

"I don't know." He looked at the minute again. "Neither does he. That's why he wanted the date on it."

---

Karis had pinned four printed sheets to the wall of carrel eleven at the height of her eyes, and she would not say what they were until Lira had shut the door.

"Appeals," she said. "The chancellor writes one every year to the academy's old students and friends, asking for money. These are the last four. Read the second paragraph of each, left to right."

He read them. The first second paragraph talked about the school's place among its peers. The next talked about that place and added a hope. The third gave the hope a name. The fourth named it twice, once at the top of the paragraph and once again in the last line of the letter, in capitals.

*The Continental Academy Tournament.*

"Every third year," said Karis. "Any chartered academy on the register can send a side, if it can get one through the qualifying. This cycle ends this year, a long way east of here, with nobody from Halcenvane anywhere near it. Twelve months from now, the qualifying opens again for the cycle after." She touched the fourth sheet. "And this hill hasn't sent a side through qualifying in eighteen years."

Lira was sitting on the desk with her boots on the chair. She had gone very still.

"How do they pick the side?" she said.

"Off the ladder. It's in the appendix to the charter, two floors down. I read it on Tuesday." Karis pulled the folded blanket off the chair, sat, and drew it round her shoulders, because the law range had no stove and it was cold at the top of the building. "Now look at the second sheet. Six years ago."

The last line of the second appeal asked for gifts *toward the instruments by which a school is known.*

"The assessment wing," said Karis. "They pulled it down to its footings six years ago and built it up again. The new wall, the new gate, every piece of apparatus behind those cloths. It cost more than the library roof, and the money came from that appeal." She looked at Cael. "You wanted to know why they took you. You've been asking the buildings for two weeks. Here's what the buildings say."

He stood in front of the four sheets. He did not need her to say the rest, and she did not.

A school that wanted a tournament side needed standings, and to be trusted with standings it needed a name for running things properly. And here was the hardest thing to run properly that anyone had sent to any school in a generation. It was him: an enrollment with the Compact's eyes on it, a public ruling, and a panel every semester with an empty chair kept for the Compact. If Halcenvane could carry that without a single slip, in plain view, it could be trusted to carry a side. He was not a gamble the chancellor had taken. He was a proof the chancellor meant to show.

"That's a hard way to be wanted," he said at last.

"It's a way you can check," said Karis. Her voice was gentler than her words. "I'm not telling you it's the only reason. I'm telling you it's the one written on a wall. People will give you other reasons in nicer rooms. Hold each of them up against these four sheets and see which one bends."

Lira slid off the desk.

"Then they want winners," she said.

They both turned.

"If the side comes off the ladder," said Lira slowly, "and the ladder's what that whole yard was dug out to stare down at, then this school wants winners more than it wants anything. More than it wants its Copper column tidy." Her eyes had gone bright and hard. "Good. I've got a date on a sheet."

---

The chancellor sent for him on the nineteenth day, to the long room on the first floor where the crowns were kept.

There was a case of glass on a stone plinth along one wall, and in it three crowns on three dark cushions with a brass plate under each. The Copper was the plainest of them, and the darkest, and worn bright along its top edge where a great many hands had lifted it. Its plate had been lengthened twice with new brass riveted on below the old.

Withrow was standing at the case when he came in. She was a broad woman somewhere past sixty, with white hair cropped very short and a pair of spectacles pushed up into it. She was holding a single sheet of paper.

"I'm told you take buildings apart," she said, without turning round.

"I look at them."

"Bracken warned me. He thinks it's a good sign. I've not made up my mind." She turned. "Ask me your question before I say anything else. If I start first, you'll never get it in."

"Why did you take me?"

She did not answer it in words. She held out the sheet.

It was ruled down the middle. She had written in a broad square hand on both sides of the rule, and he read it standing in front of the case with the three crowns at his shoulder.

The left column was what he would cost her. An inspection, sooner than her charter's cycle required, from an office with power over that charter, asking questions it would choose and she would not. Then a public failure, if the enrollment failed, which would be counted as Halcenvane's failure and not the clause's. Withrow noted in the margin that a tournament committee did not forget a school that dropped a thing in front of the whole registry, not for ten years at the least. And the plain running cost of him: a full panel every semester, faculty hours, apparatus, and the whole of Bracken's winter, which by rights had belonged to four hundred and twelve other people.

The right column was what he would bring her. A name, among the registry's clerks, for being the school whose paperwork held when the hardest case on the continent leaned on it. A serious practitioner, which was worth having on any hill whatever his file said. And something to prove things with, since she had a great deal to prove and, at a school of her size, very little to prove it with.

He read both columns twice. Then he gave the sheet back.

"That's three and three," he said. "They're honest. They're the ones you'd give your council." He hesitated, and then said it anyway. "But they're not why."

Withrow looked at him over the paper for some time, as if he had added up one of her columns and found it a copper short.

"No," she said. "There's a fourth line. I don't write it on the sheet." She folded the paper in half. "I always say it last. Whatever you say first, people assume you rehearsed."

She turned back to the case, and spoke to the Copper Crown on its cushion rather than to him.

"I read the hearing transcript four times. Not the summary — the transcript, all sessions, all exhibits. The law is on your side of it. I spent twenty years inside that registry. I know exactly how rare that is."

Below the tall windows the Crown yard lay empty in the afternoon. Far off across the bluff a bell marked the change of session. Neither of them moved.

"I'll tell you how rare," said Withrow, still to the case. "I could count the times on one hand and have fingers left, and I filed the other kind by the hundred. I put sound law in drawers for twenty years because it was inconvenient to the people above me. I was good at it." She turned round. "Nobody can make me put anything in a drawer now. And I mean to have done something with this chair before I hand it on."

He did not say anything. He could not think of anything that would be the right size.

"You'll want to know whether that's ambition or belief," said Withrow. "Don't ask me which. They've lived in the same coat for forty years, and I couldn't tell you where one stops. Count on both." She pulled the spectacles down onto her nose. "Was there anything else?"

"The inspection," he said. "On your sheet you wrote *sooner*."

"Five years between inspections, by my charter. The last was two years ago." She was already looking past him at the door. "Do your own sum. I'd like it to be later. It won't be."

---

The assessment office's notice was in his pigeonhole on the twenty-first morning, a half-sheet folded once, in a clerk's hand he did not know.

*Assay-provision enrollee. To demonstrate for entry before the assessment office's panel, Magister Gault presiding. The office will fix the day within the month and post it two days before. The enrollee will hold himself available from the first bell. The demonstration establishes the baseline against which all subsequent evaluations are measured.*

He read it in the cloister by the pigeonholes, and then read the last sentence again on its own.

Bracken had told him across a bare desk. It was a different thing to have it in a clerk's hand, folded, in his own pigeonhole, with a month on it. Whatever he did on that day would be written down and kept, and every semester after it somebody would lay his new numbers beside the old ones and look for the difference. He would be measured against himself for as long as he was here, and the first measure was the one he would carry the whole way.

He found the others on the wall at dusk.

Karis had brought three books out of carrel eleven to read in the last of the light. Brom sat with his back against the coping, his new wraps across his knees, saying nothing about the east hall, though by now his silence about it had begun to be loud. Lira was leaning on the stone with her eyes on the ferry landing across the water, where two grey coats were standing under a lamp and two more were coming up the river street to take their places.

"Four hours," said Lira. "To the bell. I've had them all week."

He passed the notice along the wall. Lira read it and gave it to Brom. Brom read it slowly and gave it to Karis. Karis read it twice, folded it on its crease, and handed it back.

"Baseline," she said. "Think about what you put in it."

"I know."

"No, I mean it." She did not look up from her book. "Whatever you show first is where they measure growing from. Show too little and every semester after looks like a miracle, and somebody will want to know where miracles come from. Show too much and you've left yourself nowhere to go." She turned a page. "It's the only paper you'll ever sign that can't be amended."

Nobody said anything after that for a long while.

Four of them sat on a wall above a river at the end of a day, as they had sat on a good many walls in a good many towns. For the first time, each of them had a road the other three were not on. Lira had a bracket and a queue and a date written on a sheet. Brom had a cohort he would not talk about yet. Karis had a carrel with a key and three floors of law below it. Cael had half a sheet of paper and a month. They had come through every gate together and would go on coming through them together. But past this one the roads ran four ways at once, and all of them could feel it.

He took the binder out and wrote the day while there was light enough.

*Twenty-one days. Why they took me, as near as I'll get it this side of a year.*

*At Greyvane two people believed something about me and put their names under it. Quenna bet her standing, and Naveth his steadiness on a witness floor, and between them they held a roof over me through a winter. That roof stood on two people. When I walked out of their gate I walked out from under it. It's theirs. It didn't come with me.*

*Here a woman showed me a ruled sheet with what I cost her on the left and what I'm worth to her on the right, and then told me the one line she leaves off it. Her school will be inspected on my account. She put that at the top of the sheet.*

*So: which is the better roof? Belief rests on the people holding it up, and people get tired, or leave, or die. A reason rests on the sum staying in its favour, and I can see this sum. It's four boards on a wall, a tournament next year, and a wing built with money somebody begged for. I can climb up and look at a reason's beams. I can't climb inside a person.*

He read that back. Then he wrote the rest, because a binder that kept only the comfortable half was no use to him.

*I don't believe it. Not all the way. A reason holds until the day it costs more than it brings, and then it's gone, quickly, with a ledger to show why. Belief is whatever's left in the chair after the sum has turned. Quenna's sum turned against her in that hall in front of two hundred people, and she stayed in the chair one row back, exactly where I'd asked her to sit.*

*The first kind of roof I can climb up and check. The second I can only live under. I have. It didn't fall in.*

*Not settled. Filed. Look again in a year.*

He closed the binder. Across the river the waterfront lamps had come up along the whole length of Ostrand, and under the lamp at the ferry landing the two new grey coats had settled in for their four hours. Behind the four of them, in the dark hollow at the middle of the bluff, the boards stood on their wall with last season's names wiped clean, and nobody yet had chalked up this season's.

